1
00:01:14,908 --> 00:01:16,967
My dad was a farmer.

2
00:01:18,412 --> 00:01:20,608
Like everybody else back then.

3
00:01:22,749 --> 00:01:24,911
Of course, he didn't start that way.

4
00:01:30,624 --> 00:01:32,285
Computer says you're too tight.

5
00:01:32,593 --> 00:01:34,083
Nah, I got this.

6
00:01:34,328 --> 00:01:35,853
Crossing the Straights.

7
00:01:38,265 --> 00:01:39,994
-Shutting it down, Cooper.
-No!

8
00:01:41,501 --> 00:01:43,936
-Shutting it all down.
-Whoa, I need power up!

9
00:01:48,275 --> 00:01:49,299
Dad?

10
00:01:55,882 --> 00:01:58,374
Sorry, Murph. Go back to bed.

11
00:01:58,719 --> 00:02:01,211
-I thought you were the ghost.
-No.

12
00:02:02,856 --> 00:02:04,984
There are no such things
as ghosts, babe.

13
00:02:05,192 --> 00:02:07,217
Grandpa says you can get ghosts.

14
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:13,396
That's because Grandpa's close
to being one himself. Back to bed.

15
00:02:13,734 --> 00:02:15,566
Were you dreaming about the crash?

16
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:19,268
Get your butt back in bed, Murph.

17
00:02:46,299 --> 00:02:47,789
The wheat had died.

18
00:02:48,635 --> 00:02:50,967
The blight came and we had to burn it.

19
00:02:51,872 --> 00:02:54,569
And we still had corn.
We had acres of corn.

20
00:02:54,775 --> 00:02:57,005
But mostly we had dust.

21
00:02:59,446 --> 00:03:01,938
I can't describe it. It was just constant.

22
00:03:02,215 --> 00:03:04,650
Just that steady blow...

23
00:03:04,851 --> 00:03:06,080
...of dirt.

24
00:03:08,188 --> 00:03:11,180
We wore little strips of sheet...

25
00:03:11,458 --> 00:03:14,120
...sometimes over our nose and mouth...

26
00:03:14,327 --> 00:03:16,819
...so that we wouldn't breathe
so much of it.

27
00:03:18,532 --> 00:03:20,967
When we set the table,
we always set the plate...

28
00:03:21,168 --> 00:03:24,069
...upside-down.
Glasses or cups, whatever it was...

29
00:03:24,271 --> 00:03:25,466
...upside-down.

30
00:03:30,877 --> 00:03:33,073
Shake a leg! Murph, get a move on!

31
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:36,716
Tom, 4:00 today, you and me
in the barn, Herbicide Resistance 101.

32
00:03:36,917 --> 00:03:38,407
-Check?
-Yes, sir.

33
00:03:39,986 --> 00:03:41,613
Not at the table, Murph.

34
00:03:42,389 --> 00:03:44,221
Dad, can you fix this?

35
00:03:45,826 --> 00:03:48,318
-What the heck did you do to my lander?
-Wasn't me.

36
00:03:48,528 --> 00:03:50,496
Let me guess. It was your ghost?

37
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:52,995
It knocked it off.
It keeps knocking books off.

38
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:57,000
-No such thing as ghosts, dumb-ass.
-I looked it up. It's called a poltergeist.

39
00:03:57,204 --> 00:03:58,262
Dad, tell her.

40
00:03:58,505 --> 00:04:00,439
It's not very scientific, Murph.

41
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:03,041
You said science was about admitting
what we don't know.

42
00:04:03,276 --> 00:04:04,710
She's got you there.

43
00:04:05,345 --> 00:04:07,006
Start looking after our stuff.

44
00:04:08,582 --> 00:04:09,811
Coop.

45
00:04:12,619 --> 00:04:14,781
All right, Murph,
you want to talk science?

46
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:19,056
Don't tell me you're afraid of some
ghost. No, you got to go further.

47
00:04:19,259 --> 00:04:22,320
You got to record the facts, analyze,
get to the how and the why...

48
00:04:22,529 --> 00:04:25,430
...then present your conclusions. Deal?

49
00:04:26,099 --> 00:04:27,533
-Deal.
-All right.

50
00:04:30,337 --> 00:04:32,533
-Have a good day at school.
-Hold up.

51
00:04:32,839 --> 00:04:35,274
Parent-teacher conferences. Parent.

52
00:04:35,475 --> 00:04:37,239
Not grandparent.

53
00:04:39,546 --> 00:04:41,344
Slow down, turbo!

54
00:04:42,682 --> 00:04:45,777
-That's not a dust storm.
-Nelson's torching his whole crop.

55
00:04:46,052 --> 00:04:47,349
Blight?

56
00:04:47,554 --> 00:04:49,955
They're saying
it's the last harvest for okra.

57
00:04:50,624 --> 00:04:51,785
Ever.

58
00:04:53,693 --> 00:04:56,355
He should've planted corn
like the rest of us.

59
00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:59,156
Now, be nice to that Miss Hanley.
She's single.

60
00:04:59,366 --> 00:05:00,856
What's that supposed to mean?

61
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:04,162
Repopulating the earth.
Start pulling your weight, young man.

62
00:05:04,371 --> 00:05:08,308
Why don't you start
minding your own business, old man?

63
00:05:15,615 --> 00:05:17,276
All right, Murph, give me second.

64
00:05:20,687 --> 00:05:22,052
Third.

65
00:05:23,623 --> 00:05:25,250
-Find a gear, dumb-ass.
-Grind it!

66
00:05:25,458 --> 00:05:26,482
Shut it, Tom!

67
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:30,159
-What'd you do, Murph?
-She didn't do nothing.

68
00:05:30,363 --> 00:05:32,855
-Blew a tire is all.
-Murphy's Law.

69
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:34,758
Shut up!

70
00:05:34,968 --> 00:05:36,959
-Grab the spare, Tom.
-That is the spare.

71
00:05:39,606 --> 00:05:41,096
Get the patch kit!

72
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:45,538
-How am I supposed to patch it here?
-You got to figure it out.

73
00:05:45,745 --> 00:05:47,713
I'm not always
gonna be here to help you.

74
00:05:56,156 --> 00:05:57,988
What's going on, Murph?

75
00:05:58,225 --> 00:06:01,217
Why did you and Mom name me
after something that's bad?

76
00:06:01,494 --> 00:06:02,825
We didn't.

77
00:06:03,196 --> 00:06:04,686
Murphy's Law?

78
00:06:05,665 --> 00:06:09,659
Murphy's Law doesn't mean
that something bad will happen.

79
00:06:10,003 --> 00:06:13,337
What it means is
whatever can happen will happen.

80
00:06:13,673 --> 00:06:16,005
And that sounded just fine with us.

81
00:06:22,349 --> 00:06:23,612
Get in.

82
00:06:25,018 --> 00:06:26,417
Get in, let's go.

83
00:06:27,754 --> 00:06:29,882
What about the flat tire?

84
00:06:32,859 --> 00:06:34,190
Yeah.

85
00:06:48,141 --> 00:06:52,100
It's an Indian Air Force drone.
Solar cells could power an entire farm.

86
00:06:52,312 --> 00:06:53,780
Take the wheel, Tom.

87
00:06:59,252 --> 00:07:00,583
Go, go, go!

88
00:07:02,155 --> 00:07:03,816
Keep that aimed right at it.

89
00:07:11,765 --> 00:07:14,166
Faster, Tom. I'm losing it.

90
00:07:14,868 --> 00:07:15,892
Right at it.

91
00:07:17,404 --> 00:07:18,428
Stay on it.

92
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:21,098
Here we go.

93
00:07:30,383 --> 00:07:31,942
Nice one, Tom.

94
00:07:38,458 --> 00:07:39,789
Dad?

95
00:07:40,226 --> 00:07:42,957
I almost got it. Don't stop. Don't stop!

96
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:44,454
Dad!

97
00:07:44,798 --> 00:07:45,890
Tom!

98
00:07:50,570 --> 00:07:52,902
You told me to keep driving.

99
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:58,177
I guess that answers the old "if I asked
you to drive off a cliff" scenario.

100
00:08:01,381 --> 00:08:04,214
-We lost it.
-No, we didn't.

101
00:08:16,930 --> 00:08:18,591
Want to give it a whirl?

102
00:08:24,604 --> 00:08:25,969
This way.

103
00:08:31,344 --> 00:08:32,436
Go.

104
00:08:33,446 --> 00:08:36,438
Let's lay her down right there
at the edge of the reservoir.

105
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:42,449
Nicely done.

106
00:08:45,425 --> 00:08:47,291
How long you think it's been up there?

107
00:08:47,494 --> 00:08:49,963
Delhi Mission Control went down,
same as ours...

108
00:08:50,163 --> 00:08:51,653
...ten years ago.

109
00:08:52,365 --> 00:08:54,299
So for ten years?

110
00:08:57,437 --> 00:08:59,838
-Why did it come down so low?
-I don't know.

111
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:02,941
Maybe the sun cooked its brain
or it was looking for something.

112
00:09:03,143 --> 00:09:05,373
-What?
-Give me a large flatblade.

113
00:09:05,812 --> 00:09:08,474
Maybe some kind of signal?
I don't know.

114
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:15,210
What are you gonna do with it?

115
00:09:16,156 --> 00:09:19,319
I'm gonna give it something
socially responsible to do...

116
00:09:20,193 --> 00:09:21,524
...like drive a combine.

117
00:09:22,095 --> 00:09:23,585
Can't we just let it go?

118
00:09:25,899 --> 00:09:27,560
It wasn't hurting anybody.

119
00:09:29,235 --> 00:09:31,727
This thing needs to learn how to adapt,
Murph...

120
00:09:32,038 --> 00:09:33,528
...like the rest of us.

121
00:09:38,211 --> 00:09:41,044
-How's this work? You guys come with?
-I've got class.

122
00:09:41,448 --> 00:09:43,815
This one needs to wait.

123
00:09:45,385 --> 00:09:48,116
-What did you do?
-They'll tell you about it in there.

124
00:09:48,321 --> 00:09:50,415
-Am I gonna be mad?
-Not with me.

125
00:09:50,623 --> 00:09:53,820
-Just please try not to.
-Hey. Relax.

126
00:09:55,261 --> 00:09:56,592
I got this.

127
00:10:00,834 --> 00:10:02,131
Little late, Coop.

128
00:10:02,368 --> 00:10:03,858
Yeah, we had a flat.

129
00:10:04,137 --> 00:10:07,573
And I guess you had to stop off
at the Asian fighter plane store.

130
00:10:07,874 --> 00:10:11,367
No, actually, sir,
that's a surveillance drone.

131
00:10:11,644 --> 00:10:14,341
With outstanding solar cells. It's Indian.

132
00:10:14,781 --> 00:10:16,112
Take a seat.

133
00:10:17,250 --> 00:10:18,740
So...

134
00:10:19,452 --> 00:10:22,046
...we got Tom's scores back.

135
00:10:22,589 --> 00:10:24,580
He's going to make an excellent farmer.

136
00:10:24,924 --> 00:10:28,155
Yeah, he's got a knack for it.
What about college?

137
00:10:28,428 --> 00:10:32,058
The university only takes a handful.
They don't have the resources to--

138
00:10:32,265 --> 00:10:34,256
I still pay my taxes.

139
00:10:34,501 --> 00:10:36,765
Where's that money go?
There's no more armies.

140
00:10:37,270 --> 00:10:39,602
Well, it doesn't go to the university.

141
00:10:40,173 --> 00:10:41,834
Look, you have to be realistic.

142
00:10:42,041 --> 00:10:44,772
You're ruling my son out
for college now?

143
00:10:45,311 --> 00:10:48,804
-The kid's 15.
-Tom's score simply isn't high enough.

144
00:10:49,816 --> 00:10:51,978
What's your waistline? About what, 32?

145
00:10:52,385 --> 00:10:56,049
-About a 33 inseam?
-I'm not sure I see what you're getting at.

146
00:10:56,256 --> 00:11:00,659
Takes two numbers to measure your ass
but one to measure my son's future?

147
00:11:01,528 --> 00:11:04,361
Come on.
You're a well-educated man, Coop.

148
00:11:04,597 --> 00:11:06,531
-And a trained pilot.
-And an engineer.

149
00:11:07,033 --> 00:11:11,800
Okay, well, right now
we don't need more engineers.

150
00:11:12,238 --> 00:11:16,573
We didn't run out of television screens
and planes. We ran out of food.

151
00:11:16,910 --> 00:11:20,175
The world needs farmers.
Good farmers, like you.

152
00:11:20,380 --> 00:11:22,872
-And Tom.
-Uneducated farmers.

153
00:11:23,816 --> 00:11:27,616
We're a caretaker generation, Coop.
And things are getting better.

154
00:11:27,854 --> 00:11:31,017
-Maybe your grandkids will get to be--
-Are we done here, sir?

155
00:11:32,325 --> 00:11:33,383
No.

156
00:11:34,227 --> 00:11:36,389
Miss Hanley's here to talk about Murph.

157
00:11:36,729 --> 00:11:39,221
Murph is a great kid. She's really bright.

158
00:11:39,465 --> 00:11:41,627
But she's been having
a little trouble lately.

159
00:11:41,901 --> 00:11:45,201
She brought this to show the students.
The section on lunar landings.

160
00:11:45,405 --> 00:11:47,897
Yeah, it's one of my old textbooks.

161
00:11:48,374 --> 00:11:50,342
She always loved the pictures.

162
00:11:50,543 --> 00:11:54,207
It's an old federal textbook. We replaced
them with the corrected versions.

163
00:11:54,414 --> 00:11:55,472
Corrected?

164
00:11:55,682 --> 00:11:59,744
Explains how the Apollo missions were
faked to bankrupt the Soviet Union.

165
00:12:06,492 --> 00:12:08,426
You don't believe we went to the moon?

166
00:12:09,562 --> 00:12:11,530
It was a brilliant piece of propaganda.

167
00:12:11,731 --> 00:12:14,359
The Soviets bankrupted themselves
pouring resources...

168
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:16,934
...into rockets
and other useless machines.

169
00:12:17,136 --> 00:12:18,194
"Useless machines."

170
00:12:18,404 --> 00:12:22,466
If we don't want a repeat of the
wastefulness of the 20th century, then...

171
00:12:22,675 --> 00:12:26,441
...we need to teach our kids about
this planet, not tales of leaving it.

172
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:31,274
One of those useless machines
they used to make was called an MRI.

173
00:12:31,684 --> 00:12:34,915
If we had any of those left,
the doctors would've been able to find...

174
00:12:35,121 --> 00:12:38,887
...the cyst in my wife's brain
before she died instead of afterwards.

175
00:12:39,092 --> 00:12:42,118
Then she'd have been the one
listening to this instead of me...

176
00:12:42,328 --> 00:12:45,423
...which would have been a good thing,
because she was always...

177
00:12:46,032 --> 00:12:47,522
...the calmer one.

178
00:12:48,167 --> 00:12:50,261
I'm sorry about your wife, Mr. Cooper.

179
00:12:51,504 --> 00:12:55,168
But Murph got into a fistfight with
several of her classmates over this...

180
00:12:55,408 --> 00:12:57,001
...Apollo nonsense.

181
00:12:57,210 --> 00:12:59,440
We thought it best
to bring you in and see what ideas...

182
00:12:59,646 --> 00:13:02,479
...you might have
for dealing with her behavior.

183
00:13:03,683 --> 00:13:06,675
You know what?
There's a game tomorrow night.

184
00:13:06,953 --> 00:13:09,012
She's going through
a baseball phase.

185
00:13:09,222 --> 00:13:13,420
Her favorite team is playing.
There will be candy and soda and...

186
00:13:16,496 --> 00:13:18,328
I think I'll take her to that.

187
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:24,933
How'd it go?

188
00:13:25,838 --> 00:13:27,738
I got you suspended.

189
00:13:28,141 --> 00:13:29,472
-What?
-Cooper.

190
00:13:31,210 --> 00:13:32,371
This is Cooper.

191
00:13:32,578 --> 00:13:35,343
Coop, those combines you rebuilt
went haywire.

192
00:13:35,581 --> 00:13:37,743
Just reset the controllers.

193
00:13:38,084 --> 00:13:40,678
I did that. You should come take a look.

194
00:14:00,406 --> 00:14:03,376
One by one they've been
peeling off the fields and heading over.

195
00:14:03,576 --> 00:14:05,442
Something's interfering
with the compass.

196
00:14:06,913 --> 00:14:09,314
Magnetism or some such.

197
00:14:52,225 --> 00:14:53,886
Nothing special about which book.

198
00:14:54,560 --> 00:14:56,824
I've been working on it, like you said.

199
00:14:57,563 --> 00:15:00,055
-I counted the spaces.
-Why?

200
00:15:02,101 --> 00:15:04,195
In case the ghost
is trying to communicate.

201
00:15:04,971 --> 00:15:06,530
I'm trying Morse.

202
00:15:07,106 --> 00:15:08,369
Morse?

203
00:15:08,708 --> 00:15:09,766
Yeah.

204
00:15:10,209 --> 00:15:12,701
-Dots and dashes, used--
-I know what Morse code is.

205
00:15:13,012 --> 00:15:15,913
I just don't think your bookshelf's
trying to talk to you.

206
00:15:27,493 --> 00:15:31,896
Had to reset every compass clock
and GPS to offset for the anomaly.

207
00:15:32,198 --> 00:15:34,724
-Which is?
-I don't know.

208
00:15:36,502 --> 00:15:38,596
If the house was built on magnetic ore...

209
00:15:38,805 --> 00:15:41,900
...we'd have seen this the first time
we switched on the tractor.

210
00:15:44,577 --> 00:15:47,740
I hear your meeting at the school
didn't go so well.

211
00:15:49,048 --> 00:15:50,072
You heard?

212
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:53,883
It's like we've forgotten
who we are, Donald.

213
00:15:55,555 --> 00:15:59,048
Explorers, pioneers, not caretakers.

214
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:02,228
When I was a kid...

215
00:16:02,428 --> 00:16:06,661
...it felt like they made something new
every day. Some...

216
00:16:07,033 --> 00:16:10,333
...gadget or idea.
Like every day was Christmas.

217
00:16:10,803 --> 00:16:13,465
But six billion people...

218
00:16:13,906 --> 00:16:15,897
...just try to imagine that.

219
00:16:16,909 --> 00:16:19,901
And every last one of them
trying to have it all.

220
00:16:23,182 --> 00:16:25,173
This world isn't so bad.

221
00:16:27,453 --> 00:16:29,285
And Tom will do just fine.

222
00:16:29,555 --> 00:16:34,186
You're the one who doesn't belong.
Born 40 years too late, or too early.

223
00:16:35,194 --> 00:16:37,686
My daughter knew it, God bless her.

224
00:16:38,564 --> 00:16:41,363
And your kids know it. Especially Murph.

225
00:16:41,701 --> 00:16:45,069
Well, we used to look up in the sky
and wonder...

226
00:16:45,338 --> 00:16:47,670
...at our place in the stars.

227
00:16:50,076 --> 00:16:54,479
Now we just look down and worry
about our place in the dirt.

228
00:16:54,981 --> 00:16:57,814
Cooper, you were good at something...

229
00:16:58,150 --> 00:17:00,642
...and you never got a chance
to do anything with it.

230
00:17:01,454 --> 00:17:02,785
I'm sorry.

231
00:17:04,490 --> 00:17:08,586
You didn't expect this dirt
that was giving you this food...

232
00:17:08,961 --> 00:17:12,022
...to turn on you
like that and destroy you.

233
00:17:12,465 --> 00:17:16,663
In April-- I believe I'm right--
Fifteenth of April, I think.

234
00:17:17,003 --> 00:17:19,404
This happened about 1 :30
when that thing...

235
00:17:19,739 --> 00:17:22,003
...came off the top of that canyon.

236
00:17:23,209 --> 00:17:25,200
In my day, we had real ballplayers.

237
00:17:25,611 --> 00:17:27,238
Who are these bums?

238
00:17:27,613 --> 00:17:29,980
In my day, people were too busy...

239
00:17:30,182 --> 00:17:32,344
...fighting over food
to even play baseball.

240
00:17:32,652 --> 00:17:37,146
Popcorn at a ballgame is unnatural.
I want a hot dog.

241
00:17:37,490 --> 00:17:39,822
School says you're gonna follow
in my footsteps.

242
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:42,050
I think that's great.

243
00:17:42,361 --> 00:17:45,626
-You think that's great?
-You hate farming, Dad.

244
00:17:46,432 --> 00:17:47,695
Grandpa said.

245
00:17:48,034 --> 00:17:49,627
Grandpa said, huh?

246
00:17:50,069 --> 00:17:52,504
Listen, all that matters
is how you feel about it.

247
00:17:52,772 --> 00:17:54,433
I like what you do.

248
00:17:55,141 --> 00:17:56,631
I like our farm.

249
00:17:57,343 --> 00:17:59,744
You're gonna be great at it.

250
00:18:11,791 --> 00:18:13,281
Let's get out of here.

251
00:18:20,566 --> 00:18:22,557
All right, it's a doozy.

252
00:18:23,269 --> 00:18:25,101
Gang, let's mask up.

253
00:18:28,441 --> 00:18:30,432
-Tom? Murph? Check?
-Yeah.

254
00:19:23,996 --> 00:19:26,590
Murph, Tom,
you guys shut your windows?

255
00:19:31,003 --> 00:19:32,061
Murph?

256
00:19:53,492 --> 00:19:54,516
The ghost.

257
00:19:56,028 --> 00:19:57,655
Grab your pillow.

258
00:19:58,464 --> 00:20:00,296
You're sleeping in with Tom.

259
00:20:55,221 --> 00:20:56,882
It's not a ghost.

260
00:21:02,294 --> 00:21:03,728
It's gravity.

261
00:21:05,464 --> 00:21:07,728
I'm dropping Tom,
then heading to town.

262
00:21:08,033 --> 00:21:12,197
You want to clean that up
when you've finished praying to it?

263
00:21:37,563 --> 00:21:39,793
It's not Morse, Murph. It's binary.

264
00:21:40,132 --> 00:21:42,260
Thick is one, thin is zero.

265
00:21:46,739 --> 00:21:48,070
Coordinates.

266
00:21:50,609 --> 00:21:51,633
Nope.

267
00:21:54,613 --> 00:21:55,774
Here we go.

268
00:21:57,349 --> 00:21:58,714
Thirty-three.

269
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:02,744
That's it.

270
00:22:04,190 --> 00:22:05,680
I can't miss this!

271
00:22:06,025 --> 00:22:09,188
Grandpa will be back in a couple hours,
Murph.

272
00:22:09,995 --> 00:22:12,657
But you don't know
what you're gonna find.

273
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:14,997
And that...

274
00:22:16,068 --> 00:22:18,162
...is why I can't take you.

275
00:22:24,276 --> 00:22:25,368
Murph?

276
00:22:27,479 --> 00:22:30,972
Grandpa will be home in a while.
Tell him I'll call him on the radio.

277
00:22:37,356 --> 00:22:38,380
Jesus.

278
00:22:39,391 --> 00:22:40,881
What are you doing?

279
00:22:41,493 --> 00:22:43,621
You think this is funny?

280
00:22:43,929 --> 00:22:46,261
You wouldn't be here if it wasn't for me.

281
00:22:48,133 --> 00:22:50,124
Make yourself useful.

282
00:23:53,465 --> 00:23:54,796
Hey, Murph?

283
00:23:56,435 --> 00:23:57,459
Murph!

284
00:24:00,973 --> 00:24:03,305
I think this is the end of the road.

285
00:24:06,312 --> 00:24:08,371
Didn't you bring the bolt cutters?

286
00:24:09,515 --> 00:24:10,778
That's my girl.

287
00:24:38,510 --> 00:24:39,909
Step away from the fence!

288
00:24:40,179 --> 00:24:43,513
Don't shoot! I'm not armed.
My daughter's in the car.

289
00:24:49,121 --> 00:24:50,748
Don't be afraid.

290
00:25:03,535 --> 00:25:05,629
How did you find this place?

291
00:25:06,338 --> 00:25:07,669
Where's my daughter?

292
00:25:07,973 --> 00:25:11,841
You had the coordinates for this facility
on your map. Where did you get them?

293
00:25:12,044 --> 00:25:13,341
Where's my daughter?

294
00:25:13,545 --> 00:25:16,480
Don't make me take you down again.
Sit down!

295
00:25:16,815 --> 00:25:18,909
Oh, you still think you're a Marine, pal?

296
00:25:19,184 --> 00:25:21,744
Marines don't exist anymore.

297
00:25:22,054 --> 00:25:26,992
- I got grunts like you mowing my grass.
-Where did you find those coordinates?

298
00:25:27,226 --> 00:25:29,718
But you don't look much
like a lawnmower.

299
00:25:29,928 --> 00:25:32,761
Think I'll turn you into an overqualified
vacuum cleaner.

300
00:25:32,965 --> 00:25:34,126
No, you won't.

301
00:25:35,934 --> 00:25:37,698
TARS, back down, please.

302
00:25:39,204 --> 00:25:42,333
You know, you're taking a risk
using ex-military security.

303
00:25:42,808 --> 00:25:46,472
They're old, and their control units
are unpredictable.

304
00:25:46,779 --> 00:25:48,770
It's what the government could spare.

305
00:25:50,482 --> 00:25:51,643
Who are you?

306
00:25:52,084 --> 00:25:53,415
Dr. Brand.

307
00:25:53,619 --> 00:25:55,951
I knew a Dr. Brand once.
He was a professor.

308
00:25:56,355 --> 00:25:57,845
What makes you think I'm not?

309
00:25:58,323 --> 00:26:00,655
Wasn't near as cute, either.

310
00:26:00,993 --> 00:26:03,485
Please, Dr. Brand.
I don't have any idea what this is.

311
00:26:04,329 --> 00:26:06,764
I'm scared for my daughter
and want her by my side.

312
00:26:06,965 --> 00:26:09,434
Give me that,
I'll tell you anything you want to know.

313
00:26:12,805 --> 00:26:15,797
Get the principals and the girl
in the conference room, please.

314
00:26:17,142 --> 00:26:18,541
Your daughter is fine.

315
00:26:18,977 --> 00:26:20,001
Bright kid.

316
00:26:20,612 --> 00:26:22,410
Must have a very smart mother.

317
00:26:22,614 --> 00:26:25,015
It's pretty clear
you don't want any visitors.

318
00:26:25,250 --> 00:26:29,153
Why don't you just let us back up from
your fence and we'll be on our way?

319
00:26:29,455 --> 00:26:32,015
-It's not that simple.
-Sure, it is.

320
00:26:32,324 --> 00:26:35,988
I don't know anything about you.
I don't know anything about this place.

321
00:26:36,895 --> 00:26:38,056
Yes, you do.

322
00:26:43,769 --> 00:26:44,827
Dad!

323
00:26:47,506 --> 00:26:48,530
Hello, Cooper.

324
00:26:50,042 --> 00:26:51,066
Professor Brand.

325
00:26:52,578 --> 00:26:55,172
Explain to me
how you found this facility.

326
00:26:55,380 --> 00:26:58,645
Kind of an accident. We stumbled
upon it. We were on a salvage run--

327
00:26:58,851 --> 00:27:01,479
You're sitting in the best-kept secret
in the world.

328
00:27:01,687 --> 00:27:05,180
Nobody stumbles in here.
Nobody stumbles out.

329
00:27:05,524 --> 00:27:07,185
Cooper, please.

330
00:27:07,659 --> 00:27:09,753
Cooperate with these people.

331
00:27:09,928 --> 00:27:11,123
Look.

332
00:27:12,064 --> 00:27:13,725
It's kind of hard to explain.

333
00:27:14,032 --> 00:27:18,230
We learned these coordinates
from an anomaly.

334
00:27:18,971 --> 00:27:20,268
What sort of anomaly?

335
00:27:20,472 --> 00:27:23,703
I hesitate to term it supernatural,
but it wasn't scientific.

336
00:27:23,976 --> 00:27:27,810
You're going to have to be specific,
Mr. Cooper. Right now.

337
00:27:28,013 --> 00:27:29,276
It was gravity.

338
00:27:34,386 --> 00:27:37,185
What sort of gravitational anomaly?
Where was this?

339
00:27:37,456 --> 00:27:40,585
I'm real happy that you're excited
about gravity, bud...

340
00:27:40,792 --> 00:27:43,591
...but you're not getting any answers
until I get assurances.

341
00:27:43,795 --> 00:27:45,456
-Assurances?
-Yeah.

342
00:27:46,298 --> 00:27:49,791
That we're getting out of here. And
I don't mean in the trunk of some car.

343
00:27:52,304 --> 00:27:54,398
Don't you know who we are, Coop?

344
00:27:54,606 --> 00:27:55,801
No, professor, I don't.

345
00:27:56,008 --> 00:27:57,999
You know my father, Professor Brand.

346
00:27:58,644 --> 00:27:59,907
We're NASA.

347
00:28:01,713 --> 00:28:03,909
-NASA?
-NASA.

348
00:28:04,249 --> 00:28:05,978
The same NASA you flew for.

349
00:28:17,095 --> 00:28:21,054
I heard they shut you down for refusing
to drop bombs from the stratosphere...

350
00:28:21,266 --> 00:28:22,893
...onto starving people.

351
00:28:23,101 --> 00:28:25,399
When they realized killing
other people was...

352
00:28:25,604 --> 00:28:28,403
...not a long-term solution,
then they needed us back.

353
00:28:28,674 --> 00:28:30,574
-In secret.
-Why secret?

354
00:28:30,909 --> 00:28:34,641
Because public opinion wouldn't
allow spending on space exploration.

355
00:28:35,080 --> 00:28:37,378
Not while you're struggling
to put food on the table.

356
00:28:38,817 --> 00:28:39,978
Blight.

357
00:28:40,419 --> 00:28:44,253
Wheat seven years ago. Okra this year.

358
00:28:45,023 --> 00:28:46,684
Now there's just corn.

359
00:28:47,025 --> 00:28:48,925
And we're growing more
than we ever have.

360
00:28:49,127 --> 00:28:53,291
But like the potatoes in Ireland
and the wheat in the Dust Bowl...

361
00:28:53,899 --> 00:28:56,391
...the corn will die.

362
00:28:57,169 --> 00:28:58,193
Soon.

363
00:29:03,508 --> 00:29:07,069
We'll find a way, professor.
We always have.

364
00:29:07,846 --> 00:29:11,805
Driven by the unshakable faith
the Earth is ours.

365
00:29:12,317 --> 00:29:14,217
Not just ours, no.

366
00:29:14,820 --> 00:29:16,310
But it is our home.

367
00:29:17,689 --> 00:29:20,852
Earth's atmosphere
is 80 percent nitrogen.

368
00:29:21,093 --> 00:29:22,857
We don't even breathe nitrogen.

369
00:29:23,095 --> 00:29:28,090
Blight does. And as it thrives,
our air gets less and less oxygen.

370
00:29:28,567 --> 00:29:32,003
The last people to starve
will be the first to suffocate.

371
00:29:32,237 --> 00:29:34,331
And your daughter's generation...

372
00:29:36,008 --> 00:29:38,238
...will be the last to survive on Earth.

373
00:29:38,477 --> 00:29:41,970
Murph is tired. I was wondering
if she could take a nap in my office.

374
00:29:42,514 --> 00:29:44,004
Yeah. Thank you.

375
00:29:46,818 --> 00:29:47,876
Okay...

376
00:29:48,887 --> 00:29:52,380
...now you need to tell me
what your plan is to save the world.

377
00:29:52,991 --> 00:29:56,552
We're not meant to save the world.
We're meant to leave it.

378
00:30:02,367 --> 00:30:03,391
Rangers.

379
00:30:03,735 --> 00:30:08,673
The last components of our one
versatile ship in orbit, the Endurance.

380
00:30:08,974 --> 00:30:10,601
Our final expedition.

381
00:30:11,376 --> 00:30:14,346
You sent people out there
looking for a new home?

382
00:30:14,980 --> 00:30:16,277
The Lazarus missions.

383
00:30:16,615 --> 00:30:19,550
-Oh, that sounds cheerful.
-Lazarus came back from the dead.

384
00:30:19,885 --> 00:30:21,876
Sure, but he had to die in the first place.

385
00:30:22,521 --> 00:30:25,218
There's not a planet
that could sustain life...

386
00:30:25,424 --> 00:30:27,893
...and the nearest star's
over a thousand years away.

387
00:30:28,093 --> 00:30:30,755
That doesn't even qualify as futile.

388
00:30:32,397 --> 00:30:33,728
Where'd you send them?

389
00:30:34,166 --> 00:30:35,565
Cooper...

390
00:30:35,734 --> 00:30:38,362
...I can't tell you anymore
unless you agree...

391
00:30:38,570 --> 00:30:40,504
...to pilot this craft.

392
00:30:40,739 --> 00:30:44,004
-You're the best pilot we ever had.
-I barely left the stratosphere.

393
00:30:44,309 --> 00:30:46,403
This team never left the simulator.

394
00:30:46,745 --> 00:30:50,511
We need a pilot, and this is the mission
that you were trained for.

395
00:30:50,716 --> 00:30:52,206
Without even knowing it?

396
00:30:53,285 --> 00:30:57,449
An hour ago, you didn't even know
I was alive. You were going anyway.

397
00:30:57,689 --> 00:30:59,350
We had no choice.

398
00:30:59,558 --> 00:31:01,322
But something sent you here.

399
00:31:01,660 --> 00:31:04,186
-They chose you.
-Who's "they"?

400
00:31:12,337 --> 00:31:14,066
How long would I be gone?

401
00:31:14,840 --> 00:31:16,171
Hard to know.

402
00:31:16,575 --> 00:31:17,736
Years?

403
00:31:18,443 --> 00:31:19,933
I've got kids, professor.

404
00:31:20,579 --> 00:31:22,513
Get out there and save them.

405
00:31:26,017 --> 00:31:27,348
Who's "they"?

406
00:31:27,586 --> 00:31:31,352
We started detecting gravitational
anomalies almost 50 years ago.

407
00:31:31,690 --> 00:31:35,149
Mostly small distortions to our
instruments in the upper atmosphere.

408
00:31:35,360 --> 00:31:38,591
In fact, I believe
you encountered one yourself.

409
00:31:39,264 --> 00:31:41,255
Yeah, over the Straights.

410
00:31:42,267 --> 00:31:45,032
My crash.
Something tripped my fly-by-wire.

411
00:31:45,370 --> 00:31:46,701
Exactly.

412
00:31:47,539 --> 00:31:52,170
But of all these anomalies,
the most significant is this:

413
00:31:52,377 --> 00:31:53,538
Out near Saturn...

414
00:31:54,780 --> 00:31:56,874
...a disturbance of space-time.

415
00:31:58,950 --> 00:32:02,113
-Is that a wormhole?
-It appeared 48 years ago.

416
00:32:02,421 --> 00:32:04,480
And it leads where?

417
00:32:05,056 --> 00:32:06,683
Another galaxy.

418
00:32:08,126 --> 00:32:10,527
A wormhole's not a naturally occurring
phenomenon.

419
00:32:10,729 --> 00:32:12,163
Someone placed it there.

420
00:32:13,064 --> 00:32:14,122
"They"?

421
00:32:15,066 --> 00:32:19,128
And whoever they are,
they appear to be looking out for us.

422
00:32:19,604 --> 00:32:22,130
That wormhole
lets us travel to other stars.

423
00:32:22,340 --> 00:32:24,138
It came along right as we needed it.

424
00:32:24,342 --> 00:32:27,312
They've put potentially habitable worlds
right within our reach.

425
00:32:27,512 --> 00:32:31,642
-Twelve, in fact, from our initial probes.
-You sent probes into that?

426
00:32:32,150 --> 00:32:35,142
We sent people into it. Ten years ago.

427
00:32:36,488 --> 00:32:38,149
The Lazarus missions.

428
00:32:38,490 --> 00:32:39,958
Twelve possible worlds...

429
00:32:40,158 --> 00:32:42,092
...twelve Ranger launches carrying...

430
00:32:42,661 --> 00:32:45,062
...the bravest humans ever to live...

431
00:32:45,263 --> 00:32:47,891
...led by the remarkable Dr. Mann.

432
00:32:48,233 --> 00:32:51,203
Each person's landing pod
had enough life support for two years.

433
00:32:51,403 --> 00:32:53,462
They could use hibernation
to stretch that...

434
00:32:53,672 --> 00:32:56,835
...making observations on organics
over a decade or more.

435
00:32:57,108 --> 00:33:00,908
Their mission was to assess their world,
and if it showed potential...

436
00:33:01,246 --> 00:33:05,410
...then they could send out a signal, bed
down for the nap, wait to be rescued.

437
00:33:05,684 --> 00:33:07,846
And what if the world
didn't show promise?

438
00:33:08,487 --> 00:33:09,818
Hence the bravery.

439
00:33:10,689 --> 00:33:13,181
You don't have the resources
to visit all 12.

440
00:33:13,425 --> 00:33:14,517
No.

441
00:33:15,427 --> 00:33:18,158
Data transmission back through
the wormhole is rudimentary.

442
00:33:18,363 --> 00:33:23,358
Binary pings on an annual basis give us
a clue as to which worlds have potential.

443
00:33:23,635 --> 00:33:25,364
And one system shows promise.

444
00:33:25,704 --> 00:33:28,264
One. That's a bit of a long shot, isn't it?

445
00:33:29,074 --> 00:33:31,907
One system with three potential worlds?

446
00:33:32,277 --> 00:33:33,938
No long shot.

447
00:33:36,081 --> 00:33:37,207
Okay.

448
00:33:37,949 --> 00:33:40,281
So if we find a home...

449
00:33:42,921 --> 00:33:44,082
...then what?

450
00:33:44,289 --> 00:33:46,383
That's the long shot.

451
00:33:46,725 --> 00:33:49,956
There's a Plan A and a Plan B.

452
00:33:50,462 --> 00:33:53,295
Did you notice anything strange
about the launch chamber?

453
00:34:05,110 --> 00:34:08,045
This entire facility's a centrifuge.

454
00:34:08,380 --> 00:34:10,872
Some kind of vehicle. A space station?

455
00:34:11,216 --> 00:34:12,877
Both. Plan A.

456
00:34:13,184 --> 00:34:15,175
How do you get it off the ground?

457
00:34:15,387 --> 00:34:19,051
The first gravitational anomalies
changed everything.

458
00:34:19,391 --> 00:34:23,726
Suddenly we knew
that harnessing gravity was real.

459
00:34:24,062 --> 00:34:28,727
So I started working on a theory
and we started building this station.

460
00:34:29,100 --> 00:34:31,194
But you haven't solved it yet.

461
00:34:32,270 --> 00:34:33,829
That's why there's Plan B.

462
00:34:34,339 --> 00:34:39,004
The problem is gravity. Getting a viable
amount of human life off the planet.

463
00:34:39,210 --> 00:34:40,507
This is one way.

464
00:34:40,845 --> 00:34:43,337
Plan B: a population bomb.

465
00:34:43,648 --> 00:34:48,415
Over 5,000 fertilized eggs
weighing in at just under 900 kilos.

466
00:34:49,120 --> 00:34:50,588
How would you raise them?

467
00:34:50,789 --> 00:34:53,224
With equipment on board,
we incubate the first 10.

468
00:34:53,458 --> 00:34:57,292
After that, with surrogacy,
the growth becomes exponential.

469
00:34:58,029 --> 00:35:01,226
Within 30 years,
we could have a colony of hundreds.

470
00:35:01,499 --> 00:35:04,833
The real difficulty with colonization
is genetic diversity.

471
00:35:05,470 --> 00:35:06,960
This takes care of that.

472
00:35:07,305 --> 00:35:09,637
But what about the people here?
You just...

473
00:35:10,041 --> 00:35:11,372
...give up on them?

474
00:35:11,977 --> 00:35:13,240
My kids?

475
00:35:13,845 --> 00:35:17,110
That's why Plan A is a lot more fun.

476
00:35:19,384 --> 00:35:21,546
-How far have you got?
-Almost there.

477
00:35:22,320 --> 00:35:24,812
You're asking me to hang everything...

478
00:35:25,857 --> 00:35:27,188
...on an almost.

479
00:35:27,759 --> 00:35:30,057
I'm asking you to trust me.

480
00:35:33,131 --> 00:35:34,792
Find us a new home.

481
00:35:35,567 --> 00:35:37,558
And by the time you return...

482
00:35:38,136 --> 00:35:40,798
...I will have solved
the problem of gravity.

483
00:35:41,773 --> 00:35:43,263
I give you my word.

484
00:35:55,987 --> 00:35:57,477
Go away!

485
00:36:08,166 --> 00:36:09,190
Murph.

486
00:36:09,567 --> 00:36:12,559
Go! if you're leaving, just go.

487
00:36:15,373 --> 00:36:18,206
This world was never enough for you,
was it, Coop?

488
00:36:19,044 --> 00:36:22,810
What, because heading out there
is what I feel like I was born to do?

489
00:36:23,748 --> 00:36:25,580
And it excites me?

490
00:36:26,317 --> 00:36:28,718
No, that does not make it wrong.

491
00:36:28,953 --> 00:36:30,045
It might.

492
00:36:30,455 --> 00:36:33,550
Don't trust the right thing
done for the wrong reason.

493
00:36:34,325 --> 00:36:36,623
The why of the thing,
that's the foundation.

494
00:36:36,828 --> 00:36:38,887
And the foundation's solid.

495
00:36:42,067 --> 00:36:47,062
We farmers sit here every year
when the rains fail and we say:

496
00:36:47,372 --> 00:36:48,601
"Next year."

497
00:36:49,274 --> 00:36:52,938
Well, next year ain't gonna save us,
nor the one after that.

498
00:36:53,578 --> 00:36:57,913
This world's a treasure, Donald, but it's
been telling us to leave for a while now.

499
00:37:02,654 --> 00:37:06,420
Mankind was born on Earth,
it was never meant to die here.

500
00:37:09,561 --> 00:37:12,724
Tom will be all right, but you got to
make things right with Murph.

501
00:37:12,931 --> 00:37:13,989
I will.

502
00:37:14,933 --> 00:37:18,096
Without making promises
you don't know you can keep.

503
00:37:28,747 --> 00:37:30,738
You have to talk to me, Murph.

504
00:37:33,251 --> 00:37:35,310
I need to fix this before I go.

505
00:37:35,587 --> 00:37:38,420
Then I'll keep it broken
so you have to stay.

506
00:37:40,024 --> 00:37:45,019
After you kids came along, your mom
said something I never understood.

507
00:37:45,396 --> 00:37:47,023
She said, "Now...

508
00:37:48,900 --> 00:37:53,394
...we're just here
to be memories for our kids."

509
00:37:58,510 --> 00:38:01,002
And I think now I understand
what she meant.

510
00:38:04,783 --> 00:38:08,515
Once you're a parent, you're the ghost
of your children's future.

511
00:38:10,155 --> 00:38:12,317
You said ghosts didn't exist.

512
00:38:18,797 --> 00:38:20,287
That's right, Murph.

513
00:38:23,501 --> 00:38:24,832
Murph, look at me.

514
00:38:27,872 --> 00:38:30,364
I can't be your ghost right now.

515
00:38:32,177 --> 00:38:34,168
I need to exist.

516
00:38:35,814 --> 00:38:37,714
They chose me.

517
00:38:38,449 --> 00:38:42,386
Murph, they chose me. You saw.
You're the one who led me to them.

518
00:38:45,023 --> 00:38:47,515
That's exactly why you can't go.

519
00:38:50,762 --> 00:38:52,696
I figured out the message.

520
00:38:53,131 --> 00:38:56,328
-One word. Know what it is?
-Murph.

521
00:38:56,501 --> 00:38:57,832
"Stay."

522
00:38:58,203 --> 00:39:00,763
It says, "Stay," Dad.

523
00:39:01,539 --> 00:39:02,870
You don't believe me.

524
00:39:03,474 --> 00:39:06,375
Look at the books! Look at this.
It says, "Stay."

525
00:39:06,911 --> 00:39:09,573
You're not listening! It says, "Stay!"

526
00:39:09,914 --> 00:39:11,746
No, I'm coming back.

527
00:39:11,950 --> 00:39:13,281
When?

528
00:39:26,297 --> 00:39:28,732
One for you, one for me.

529
00:39:29,100 --> 00:39:32,263
When I'm up there in hypersleep, or...

530
00:39:32,604 --> 00:39:35,164
...traveling near the speed of light, or...

531
00:39:36,174 --> 00:39:38,268
...near a black hole...

532
00:39:38,743 --> 00:39:43,271
...time's gonna change for me.
It's gonna run more slowly.

533
00:39:44,883 --> 00:39:46,681
When we get back...

534
00:39:47,785 --> 00:39:49,184
...we're gonna compare.

535
00:39:49,387 --> 00:39:52,015
-Time will run differently for us?
-Yeah.

536
00:39:53,091 --> 00:39:57,756
By the time I get back, we might even
be the same age, you and me.

537
00:39:58,162 --> 00:40:00,494
"What?" Imagine that!

538
00:40:05,036 --> 00:40:06,128
Aw, Murph...

539
00:40:06,404 --> 00:40:08,964
You have no idea
when you're coming back.

540
00:40:10,775 --> 00:40:12,265
No idea at all!

541
00:40:15,413 --> 00:40:17,404
Murph, don't make me leave like this.

542
00:40:17,715 --> 00:40:19,205
Come on, Murph!

543
00:40:19,717 --> 00:40:22,049
Don't make me leave like this, Murph!

544
00:40:29,661 --> 00:40:30,685
Hey...

545
00:40:31,229 --> 00:40:32,560
...I love you.

546
00:40:32,931 --> 00:40:34,831
Forever. You hear me?

547
00:40:35,300 --> 00:40:38,531
I love you forever, and I'm coming back.

548
00:40:44,309 --> 00:40:45,640
I'm coming back.

549
00:41:03,895 --> 00:41:06,057
-How'd it go?
-Fine.

550
00:41:06,364 --> 00:41:07,695
Just fine.

551
00:41:14,739 --> 00:41:15,831
I love you, Tom.

552
00:41:16,407 --> 00:41:18,501
-Travel safe, huh?
-Yeah.

553
00:41:18,676 --> 00:41:20,940
Look after our place for me, all right?

554
00:41:21,112 --> 00:41:22,341
All right?

555
00:41:27,251 --> 00:41:30,619
-Can I use your truck while you're gone?
-You mean your truck?

556
00:41:31,856 --> 00:41:33,756
I'll make sure they bring it back.

557
00:41:35,860 --> 00:41:37,658
Look after my kids, Donald!

558
00:41:50,141 --> 00:41:53,771
Go for main engine start. T-minus 10...

559
00:41:54,412 --> 00:41:56,471
-Dad!
-...nine...

560
00:41:56,681 --> 00:41:57,944
Dad!

561
00:41:58,583 --> 00:41:59,607
...eight...

562
00:42:00,952 --> 00:42:01,976
...seven...

563
00:42:02,186 --> 00:42:03,449
Dad!

564
00:42:03,755 --> 00:42:04,813
...six...

565
00:42:06,190 --> 00:42:09,091
...five, main engine start, four...

566
00:42:10,461 --> 00:42:11,622
...three...

567
00:42:12,296 --> 00:42:13,388
...two...

568
00:42:14,332 --> 00:42:16,391
...one. Booster ignition and...

569
00:42:25,877 --> 00:42:28,346
All engines look good.
Beginning roll program.

570
00:42:32,617 --> 00:42:35,450
Prepare for stage one separation.

571
00:42:35,787 --> 00:42:36,811
Stage one.

572
00:42:42,794 --> 00:42:44,626
There is Mach 1.

573
00:42:45,596 --> 00:42:49,328
Everybody good?
Plenty of slaves for my robot colony?

574
00:42:51,202 --> 00:42:54,502
They gave him a humor setting
so he'd fit in better with his unit.

575
00:42:54,839 --> 00:42:56,398
He thinks it relaxes us.

576
00:42:56,607 --> 00:42:59,440
A giant sarcastic robot.

577
00:43:00,311 --> 00:43:01,676
What a great idea!

578
00:43:01,879 --> 00:43:04,507
I have a cue light I can use
when I'm joking if you like.

579
00:43:04,715 --> 00:43:05,739
That'd probably help.

580
00:43:05,950 --> 00:43:09,614
You can use it to find your way back
after I blow you out the airlock.

581
00:43:10,855 --> 00:43:13,722
-What's your humor setting, TARS?
-That's 100 percent.

582
00:43:13,958 --> 00:43:15,949
Let's bring it down to 75, please.

583
00:43:16,294 --> 00:43:18,126
Stage two separation.

584
00:43:28,806 --> 00:43:30,968
All feeds going manual.

585
00:43:31,275 --> 00:43:32,674
Going manual.

586
00:43:34,979 --> 00:43:36,777
Deactivate probe heater
one, two and three.

587
00:43:36,948 --> 00:43:37,972
Check.

588
00:43:43,955 --> 00:43:46,686
-Taking control.
-This is handover to you.

589
00:43:50,862 --> 00:43:52,990
-A.D.F. check.
-Over.

590
00:43:53,331 --> 00:43:56,824
Pull thrusters back.
Fuel cells one, two, three.

591
00:43:57,168 --> 00:43:58,329
One hundred percent.

592
00:43:58,669 --> 00:44:00,000
Ex-mites.

593
00:44:09,480 --> 00:44:11,312
It's hard leaving everything.

594
00:44:11,682 --> 00:44:14,174
My kids. Your father.

595
00:44:14,819 --> 00:44:17,083
We're going to be spending
a lot of time together.

596
00:44:17,321 --> 00:44:20,347
-We should learn to talk.
-And when not to.

597
00:44:22,693 --> 00:44:24,320
Just being honest.

598
00:44:24,529 --> 00:44:26,759
I don't think you need to be that honest.

599
00:44:27,165 --> 00:44:29,497
Hey, TARS,
what's your honesty parameter?

600
00:44:29,867 --> 00:44:32,268
-Ninety percent.
-Ninety percent?

601
00:44:32,537 --> 00:44:35,529
Absolute honesty isn't always
the most diplomatic, nor the...

602
00:44:35,773 --> 00:44:38,367
...safest form of communication
with emotional beings.

603
00:44:38,609 --> 00:44:39,974
Okay.

604
00:44:40,411 --> 00:44:43,039
Ninety percent it is, Dr. Brand.

605
00:44:46,784 --> 00:44:49,981
We are coming up on the Endurance.
Twelve minutes out.

606
00:45:07,071 --> 00:45:09,062
Okay. Taking control.

607
00:45:12,577 --> 00:45:15,410
Approaching module port, 500 meters.

608
00:45:28,392 --> 00:45:29,553
It's all you, Doyle.

609
00:45:48,079 --> 00:45:50,571
Nice and easy, Doyle. Nice and easy.

610
00:45:55,753 --> 00:45:57,050
I feel good.

611
00:45:57,255 --> 00:45:58,416
Take us home.

612
00:46:06,097 --> 00:46:08,498
-Lock.
-Target locked.

613
00:46:08,699 --> 00:46:10,428
-Well done.
-Okay, helmets on.

614
00:46:10,635 --> 00:46:11,659
Good job.

615
00:46:45,136 --> 00:46:46,797
Door's not charging.

616
00:46:47,071 --> 00:46:48,561
Never mind.

617
00:47:11,462 --> 00:47:13,021
Cooper, you should have control.

618
00:47:13,364 --> 00:47:14,854
Control here.

619
00:47:16,300 --> 00:47:18,894
Communication with ring module active.

620
00:47:32,717 --> 00:47:33,878
That's initiate.

621
00:47:41,425 --> 00:47:43,917
-Are we ready to spin?
-Just a sec.

622
00:47:55,373 --> 00:47:57,705
-Hello, CASE.
-Hello, TARS.

623
00:47:58,676 --> 00:48:00,576
All right, we're all set.

624
00:48:01,045 --> 00:48:02,535
All right, let's do it.

625
00:48:31,709 --> 00:48:33,040
Thirty percent of spin.

626
00:48:39,917 --> 00:48:41,248
One G.

627
00:48:42,019 --> 00:48:44,010
How's gravity treating you back there?

628
00:48:44,321 --> 00:48:45,482
Well.

629
00:48:48,692 --> 00:48:50,421
Romilly? Hey, you okay?

630
00:48:50,628 --> 00:48:52,096
-Yeah.
-You all right?

631
00:48:52,296 --> 00:48:53,923
Yeah, I just need a minute.

632
00:48:54,365 --> 00:48:58,996
We have Dramamine in the hab pod,
or in the cryo-beds. I'll just be a sec.

633
00:48:59,203 --> 00:49:01,365
-Hey, Brand?
-Yeah?

634
00:49:01,705 --> 00:49:03,036
Bring a lot.

635
00:49:12,216 --> 00:49:14,878
Amelia, be safe.

636
00:49:15,219 --> 00:49:18,553
-Give my regards to Dr. Mann.
-I will, Dad.

637
00:49:18,789 --> 00:49:20,757
It looks good for your trajectory.

638
00:49:20,958 --> 00:49:24,292
We've calculated two years to Saturn.

639
00:49:24,895 --> 00:49:26,556
That's a lot of Dramamine.

640
00:49:27,264 --> 00:49:29,699
Look after my family, will you,
please, sir?

641
00:49:30,267 --> 00:49:33,032
We'll be waiting for you
when you get back.

642
00:49:33,571 --> 00:49:37,405
A little older, a little wiser,
but happy to see you.

643
00:49:40,244 --> 00:49:43,578
Do not go gentle into that good night

644
00:49:44,114 --> 00:49:48,278
Old age should burn and rave
at close of day

645
00:49:49,453 --> 00:49:54,391
Rage, rage against the dying of the light

646
00:49:55,292 --> 00:49:59,058
Though wise men at their end
Know dark is right

647
00:49:59,396 --> 00:50:02,297
Because their words had forked
No lightning they

648
00:50:02,700 --> 00:50:06,227
Do not go gentle into that good night

649
00:50:06,937 --> 00:50:11,272
Rage, rage against the dying of the light

650
00:50:27,858 --> 00:50:29,519
-You good?
-Yeah.

651
00:50:30,461 --> 00:50:31,951
Okay, thank you.

652
00:50:35,466 --> 00:50:36,797
Here. Pills.

653
00:50:39,537 --> 00:50:40,971
So alone.

654
00:50:41,438 --> 00:50:43,429
We have each other.
Dr. Mann had it worse.

655
00:50:43,641 --> 00:50:44,972
No, I mean them.

656
00:50:46,377 --> 00:50:49,711
It's a perfect planet,
and we won't find another one like her.

657
00:50:50,314 --> 00:50:53,545
No, it's not like looking for a new condo.

658
00:50:54,685 --> 00:50:56,847
The human race is going to be...

659
00:50:57,321 --> 00:50:58,345
...adrift...

660
00:50:58,556 --> 00:51:02,049
...desperate for a rock it can cling to
while it catches its breath.

661
00:51:02,660 --> 00:51:04,492
We need to find that rock.

662
00:51:04,828 --> 00:51:07,456
And our three prospects
are at the edge of what...

663
00:51:07,665 --> 00:51:09,565
...might sustain human life.

664
00:51:10,067 --> 00:51:12,399
Laura Miller's planet is first.

665
00:51:12,670 --> 00:51:14,832
Laura started our biology program.

666
00:51:20,010 --> 00:51:23,002
-Wolf Edmunds is here.
-Tell me about Edmunds.

667
00:51:24,748 --> 00:51:26,580
Wolf's a particle physicist.

668
00:51:28,886 --> 00:51:31,218
None of them had families, huh?

669
00:51:32,256 --> 00:51:35,089
No. No attachments. My father insisted.

670
00:51:35,292 --> 00:51:38,751
They all knew the odds against
ever seeing another human being again.

671
00:51:38,963 --> 00:51:40,897
I'm hoping we can surprise
three of them.

672
00:51:41,131 --> 00:51:43,031
-How about Mann?
-Dr. Mann?

673
00:51:43,233 --> 00:51:45,201
He's remarkable. He's the best of us.

674
00:51:45,402 --> 00:51:49,635
He inspired 11 people to follow him on
the loneliest journey in human history.

675
00:51:49,873 --> 00:51:51,466
Scientists, explorers.

676
00:51:51,675 --> 00:51:55,669
That's what I love.
You know, out there we face great odds.

677
00:51:56,180 --> 00:51:57,614
Death, but...

678
00:51:59,383 --> 00:52:00,680
...not evil.

679
00:52:00,985 --> 00:52:02,749
You don't think nature can be evil?

680
00:52:03,120 --> 00:52:05,782
No. Formidable. Frightening. But...

681
00:52:06,657 --> 00:52:08,147
...no, not evil.

682
00:52:09,893 --> 00:52:13,022
Is a lion evil
because it rips a gazelle to shreds?

683
00:52:13,530 --> 00:52:15,521
Just what we take with us, then.

684
00:52:16,100 --> 00:52:17,124
Yeah.

685
00:52:17,601 --> 00:52:19,592
This crew represents
the best of humanity.

686
00:52:19,803 --> 00:52:20,895
Even me, huh?

687
00:52:21,105 --> 00:52:23,096
You know what? We agreed, 90 percent.

688
00:52:23,340 --> 00:52:24,364
There you go.

689
00:52:24,808 --> 00:52:27,106
-Don't stay up.
-I'll be there in a minute.

690
00:52:27,378 --> 00:52:31,372
Just remember, Coop,
you are literally wasting your breath.

691
00:52:38,055 --> 00:52:41,218
Hey, TARS? Let's go over
that trajectory one more time.

692
00:52:41,592 --> 00:52:43,253
Eight months to Mars.

693
00:52:43,761 --> 00:52:46,662
Counter-orbital slingshot around.
Fourteen months to Saturn.

694
00:52:46,830 --> 00:52:48,093
Nothing's changed on that.

695
00:52:48,298 --> 00:52:50,392
Just let me ask you something.

696
00:52:52,636 --> 00:52:53,660
Dr. Brand and Edmunds--

697
00:52:53,837 --> 00:52:56,534
Why are you whispering?
They can't hear you.

698
00:52:57,675 --> 00:53:00,667
Dr. Brand and Edmunds. They close?

699
00:53:00,878 --> 00:53:02,107
I wouldn't know.

700
00:53:02,312 --> 00:53:05,304
Is that 90 percent "wouldn't know"
or 10 percent "wouldn't know"?

701
00:53:05,516 --> 00:53:07,848
I also have a discretion setting, Cooper.

702
00:53:10,087 --> 00:53:12,249
But not a poker face, Slick.

703
00:53:24,601 --> 00:53:25,864
Hey, guys.

704
00:53:26,704 --> 00:53:29,366
Dad's about to go down
for the long nap.

705
00:53:29,707 --> 00:53:32,540
So I wanted to give you an update.

706
00:53:34,278 --> 00:53:36,713
The Earth looks amazing from here.

707
00:53:38,415 --> 00:53:40,577
You can't see any of the dust.

708
00:53:42,720 --> 00:53:45,553
I really hope you guys are doing great.

709
00:53:45,889 --> 00:53:50,122
I know you'll get the message. Professor
Brand's assured me he'll get it to you.

710
00:53:50,794 --> 00:53:52,728
Know that I love you.

711
00:53:54,231 --> 00:53:56,495
-Is it him?
-I don't think so, Murph.

712
00:54:03,373 --> 00:54:05,034
You must be Donald.

713
00:54:07,244 --> 00:54:08,405
Hello, Murph.

714
00:54:09,146 --> 00:54:11,137
Why are you in my dad's truck?

715
00:54:11,782 --> 00:54:13,944
He wanted me
to bring it for your brother.

716
00:54:14,718 --> 00:54:16,880
He sent you a message.

717
00:54:19,957 --> 00:54:21,925
She's pretty upset with him for leaving.

718
00:54:22,126 --> 00:54:26,222
If you record any messages,
I can get them to transmit it to Cooper.

719
00:54:27,297 --> 00:54:29,959
Murph is a bright spark.

720
00:54:30,968 --> 00:54:33,460
Maybe I should fan the flame.

721
00:54:33,704 --> 00:54:36,196
She's already
making fools of her teachers.

722
00:54:36,473 --> 00:54:39,272
So maybe she should come
and make a fool out of you.

723
00:54:40,210 --> 00:54:43,202
-So where are they?
-Heading for Mars.

724
00:54:43,914 --> 00:54:47,908
The next time you hear from Cooper,
they'll be coming up on Saturn.

725
00:55:27,891 --> 00:55:28,983
As far as school goes...

726
00:55:29,159 --> 00:55:32,891
...the administration wants me to repeat
Plant Pathology. Which sucks.

727
00:55:33,397 --> 00:55:36,628
But they said I could start
Advanced Agriculture a year early.

728
00:55:38,402 --> 00:55:42,305
All right, I got to go, Dad.
Hope you're safe up there.

729
00:55:46,310 --> 00:55:47,744
Sorry, Coop.

730
00:55:48,111 --> 00:55:52,947
I asked Murph to say hi,
but she's as stubborn as her old man.

731
00:55:53,350 --> 00:55:55,182
I'll try again next time.

732
00:56:10,200 --> 00:56:11,690
You all right, Rom?

733
00:56:14,137 --> 00:56:15,798
This gets to me, Cooper.

734
00:56:16,006 --> 00:56:17,770
This. This.

735
00:56:18,275 --> 00:56:21,074
Millimeters of aluminum, that's it,
and then nothing...

736
00:56:21,378 --> 00:56:25,315
...out there for millions of miles
won't kill us in seconds.

737
00:56:26,216 --> 00:56:28,082
You know that some of the finest...

738
00:56:28,318 --> 00:56:32,152
...solo yachtsmen in the world
don't know how to swim?

739
00:56:32,723 --> 00:56:35,886
They don't know how.
And if they go overboard, they're done.

740
00:56:37,661 --> 00:56:39,595
We're explorers, Rom.

741
00:56:41,131 --> 00:56:42,792
This is our boat.

742
00:56:43,100 --> 00:56:44,158
Here.

743
00:57:11,228 --> 00:57:13,026
This is from the relay probe?

744
00:57:13,230 --> 00:57:15,221
It was in orbit around the wormhole.

745
00:57:15,732 --> 00:57:18,724
This is the wormhole,
and every time we'd come around...

746
00:57:18,936 --> 00:57:22,133
...we would receive images from
the other side of the foreign galaxy.

747
00:57:22,472 --> 00:57:24,566
Oh, yeah.
Like swinging a periscope around.

748
00:57:24,775 --> 00:57:25,833
Exactly.

749
00:57:26,109 --> 00:57:30,512
So we got a pretty good idea what
we're gonna find on the other side, huh?

750
00:57:31,615 --> 00:57:32,946
Navigationally.

751
00:57:34,418 --> 00:57:38,150
Guys? We'll be approaching
the wormhole in about three hours.

752
00:57:44,561 --> 00:57:45,585
Hey, Coop?

753
00:57:46,463 --> 00:57:48,192
Can we stop the spinning?

754
00:57:48,398 --> 00:57:49,422
Why?

755
00:57:49,633 --> 00:57:51,965
Because we're close enough
to see it now?

756
00:57:53,804 --> 00:57:54,965
All right.

757
00:57:55,138 --> 00:57:56,162
Thanks.

758
00:58:01,945 --> 00:58:03,936
There, that's it! That's the wormhole!

759
00:58:04,181 --> 00:58:05,842
Say it, don't spray it, Rom.

760
00:58:06,249 --> 00:58:07,546
It's a sphere.

761
00:58:07,751 --> 00:58:11,654
Of course it is.
You thought it would just be a hole?

762
00:58:12,856 --> 00:58:15,951
No, it's just that all the illustrations
I've ever seen, they...

763
00:58:16,493 --> 00:58:19,554
In the illustrations,
they're trying to show you how it works.

764
00:58:20,297 --> 00:58:24,131
So they say you want to go
from here to there.

765
00:58:24,468 --> 00:58:25,958
But it's too far, right?

766
00:58:27,204 --> 00:58:31,971
So a wormhole bends space like this,
so you can take a shortcut through...

767
00:58:32,309 --> 00:58:33,799
...a higher dimension.

768
00:58:34,011 --> 00:58:35,501
Okay, so...

769
00:58:35,846 --> 00:58:38,508
...to show that, they've turned
three-dimensional space...

770
00:58:38,682 --> 00:58:39,740
...into two dimensions...

771
00:58:39,950 --> 00:58:43,853
...which turns a wormhole
into two dimensions, a circle.

772
00:58:44,054 --> 00:58:46,318
What's a circle in three dimensions?

773
00:58:47,524 --> 00:58:49,014
-A sphere.
-Exactly.

774
00:58:49,426 --> 00:58:50,916
A spherical hole.

775
00:58:51,495 --> 00:58:54,328
But who put it there?
Who do we have to thank?

776
00:58:54,731 --> 00:58:59,396
I'm not thanking anybody until we get
out of here in one piece, Rom.

777
00:59:06,376 --> 00:59:08,037
Any trick to this, Doyle?

778
00:59:09,546 --> 00:59:11,036
No one knows.

779
00:59:18,188 --> 00:59:19,849
The others made it, right?

780
00:59:21,892 --> 00:59:23,553
At least some of them.

781
00:59:55,492 --> 00:59:58,655
Everybody ready to say goodbye
to our solar system?

782
00:59:59,563 --> 01:00:01,554
To our galaxy.

783
01:00:03,533 --> 01:00:04,557
Here we go.

784
01:00:37,134 --> 01:00:40,593
The controls won't work here.
We're passing through the bulk.

785
01:00:40,804 --> 01:00:45,207
It's space beyond our three dimensions.
All you can do is record and observe.

786
01:00:56,419 --> 01:00:57,443
What is that?

787
01:00:59,990 --> 01:01:01,651
I think it's them.

788
01:01:01,992 --> 01:01:03,824
Distorting space-time.

789
01:01:05,996 --> 01:01:07,020
Don't!

790
01:01:35,025 --> 01:01:36,254
What was that?

791
01:01:39,262 --> 01:01:40,752
The first handshake.

792
01:01:42,465 --> 01:01:43,489
We're...

793
01:01:45,202 --> 01:01:46,465
We're here.

794
01:02:06,923 --> 01:02:09,017
So the lost communications
came through.

795
01:02:09,226 --> 01:02:12,059
-How?
-The relay on this side cached them.

796
01:02:12,295 --> 01:02:15,788
So years of basic data.
No real surprises.

797
01:02:16,733 --> 01:02:20,135
Miller's site has kept pinging thumbs up,
as has Dr. Mann's.

798
01:02:20,503 --> 01:02:22,801
Edmunds' went down three years ago.

799
01:02:23,073 --> 01:02:24,199
Transmitter failure?

800
01:02:24,407 --> 01:02:27,308
Maybe. He was sending the thumbs up
right until it went dark.

801
01:02:27,510 --> 01:02:29,376
But Miller's still looks good, right?

802
01:02:29,779 --> 01:02:31,770
Because she's coming up fast.

803
01:02:32,249 --> 01:02:34,240
With one complication.

804
01:02:34,651 --> 01:02:37,621
The planet is much closer to Gargantua
than we thought.

805
01:02:37,821 --> 01:02:40,483
-Gargantua?
-It's what we're calling the black hole.

806
01:02:40,690 --> 01:02:42,818
Miller's and Dr. Mann's planets
both orbit it.

807
01:02:43,093 --> 01:02:46,324
-And Miller's is on the horizon?
-As a basketball around a hoop.

808
01:02:46,529 --> 01:02:48,497
Landing there takes us
dangerously close.

809
01:02:48,698 --> 01:02:51,929
And a black hole that big
has a huge gravitational pull.

810
01:02:54,537 --> 01:02:57,507
Look, I could swing around
that neutron star to decelerate.

811
01:02:57,707 --> 01:02:59,539
No, it's not that. It's time.

812
01:02:59,743 --> 01:03:04,237
The gravity on that planet will slow our
clock compared to Earth's drastically.

813
01:03:04,948 --> 01:03:05,972
How bad?

814
01:03:06,249 --> 01:03:09,742
Every hour we spend
on that planet will be...

815
01:03:10,620 --> 01:03:12,782
...seven years back on Earth.

816
01:03:13,657 --> 01:03:14,681
Jesus.

817
01:03:15,058 --> 01:03:16,685
Well, that's relativity, folks.

818
01:03:18,128 --> 01:03:20,563
Well, we can't just drop down
there without...

819
01:03:20,797 --> 01:03:22,595
Cooper, we have a mission.

820
01:03:22,799 --> 01:03:25,359
Yeah, we have a mission,
and our mission Plan A is...

821
01:03:25,568 --> 01:03:28,765
...to find a planet that can habitate
the people living on Earth now.

822
01:03:28,972 --> 01:03:31,771
You can't think about your family.
You have to think bigger.

823
01:03:31,975 --> 01:03:35,138
I am thinking about my family
and millions of other families, okay?

824
01:03:35,345 --> 01:03:39,509
Plan A does not work if the people on
Earth are dead by the time we pull it off.

825
01:03:40,817 --> 01:03:42,478
No. It doesn't.

826
01:03:42,819 --> 01:03:44,480
That's why there's a Plan B.

827
01:03:46,389 --> 01:03:47,857
Okay. Cooper's right.

828
01:03:48,058 --> 01:03:51,995
We must think of time as a resource, like
oxygen or food. Going down costs us.

829
01:03:54,297 --> 01:03:55,458
All right, look.

830
01:03:56,733 --> 01:04:00,226
Dr. Mann's data is promising,
but it will take months to get there.

831
01:04:00,537 --> 01:04:02,471
And Edmunds', it's even further.

832
01:04:02,706 --> 01:04:06,870
Miller hasn't sent much, but what she
has is promising. It's water, organics...

833
01:04:07,110 --> 01:04:09,238
-You don't find that every day.
-No, you don't.

834
01:04:09,446 --> 01:04:12,006
And just think about the resources,
including time...

835
01:04:12,182 --> 01:04:14,412
...that would be spent
trying to get back here.

836
01:04:15,552 --> 01:04:16,815
-Romilly...
-Yeah?

837
01:04:17,020 --> 01:04:21,355
...how far off from Miller's planet do we
have to be to stay out of the time shift?

838
01:04:21,558 --> 01:04:23,219
Just back from the cusp.

839
01:04:23,560 --> 01:04:26,222
Which is here,
just outside of Miller's planet.

840
01:04:26,563 --> 01:04:28,258
-Right.
-Okay.

841
01:04:29,199 --> 01:04:31,964
Here's Gargantua. Here's Miller's planet.

842
01:04:32,168 --> 01:04:35,297
Instead of taking the Endurance
into orbit around Miller's planet...

843
01:04:35,505 --> 01:04:38,338
...which would conserve fuel,
but lose a lot of time...

844
01:04:38,575 --> 01:04:42,671
...what if we take a wider orbit around
Gargantua, parallel with Miller's planet...

845
01:04:42,879 --> 01:04:45,246
...outside of this time shift, to here?

846
01:04:45,448 --> 01:04:48,247
Then we take the Ranger down,
get Miller, get her samples.

847
01:04:48,451 --> 01:04:51,113
We come back, analyze, debrief.
We're in, we're out.

848
01:04:51,321 --> 01:04:53,619
We lose a little fuel
but save a lot of time.

849
01:04:53,823 --> 01:04:55,518
-That'll work.
-That's good.

850
01:04:55,725 --> 01:04:58,524
There's no time for monkey business
or chit-chat down there.

851
01:04:58,728 --> 01:05:01,129
So, TARS,
you should definitely stay here.

852
01:05:01,331 --> 01:05:03,698
CASE, you're with me.
Anyone else can stay.

853
01:05:03,900 --> 01:05:07,393
If we're talking about a couple years,
I could research gravity.

854
01:05:07,604 --> 01:05:10,539
Observations from the wormhole,
that's gold to Professor Brand.

855
01:05:10,740 --> 01:05:12,401
TARS, factor an orbit of Gargantua.

856
01:05:12,709 --> 01:05:17,237
Conserve fuel, minimize thrusting, make
sure we stay in range of Miller's planet.

857
01:05:17,447 --> 01:05:19,939
-You got it?
-I wouldn't leave you behind...

858
01:05:20,150 --> 01:05:21,174
...Dr. Brand.

859
01:05:25,889 --> 01:05:27,448
-You ready, CASE?
-Yup.

860
01:05:28,458 --> 01:05:31,894
-You don't say much, do you?
-TARS talks plenty for both of us.

861
01:05:33,096 --> 01:05:34,120
Detach.

862
01:05:41,538 --> 01:05:44,405
-Romilly, are you reading these forces?
-It's unbelievable.

863
01:05:47,710 --> 01:05:49,804
A literal heart of darkness.

864
01:05:53,983 --> 01:05:56,475
If we could just see
the collapsed star inside...

865
01:05:56,786 --> 01:05:59,619
...the singularity, yeah,
we'd solve gravity.

866
01:06:00,056 --> 01:06:03,117
-And we can't get anything from it?
-Nothing escapes that horizon.

867
01:06:03,326 --> 01:06:04,725
Not even light.

868
01:06:04,928 --> 01:06:08,489
-Answer's there, just no way to see it.
-There's Miller's planet.

869
01:06:11,167 --> 01:06:12,498
Goodbye, Ranger.

870
01:06:25,315 --> 01:06:27,477
This is fast for atmospheric entry.

871
01:06:27,784 --> 01:06:29,775
-Should we use thrusters to slow?
-No.

872
01:06:30,487 --> 01:06:33,548
I'm gonna use the Ranger's
aerodynamics to save some fuel.

873
01:06:33,857 --> 01:06:34,881
Air brake?

874
01:06:35,091 --> 01:06:38,652
-We want to get down fast, don't we?
-We want to get there in one piece.

875
01:06:39,095 --> 01:06:42,087
-Hang on.
-Brand, Doyle, get ready.

876
01:06:57,947 --> 01:07:00,075
-We should ease!
-Hands where I can see them.

877
01:07:00,283 --> 01:07:03,583
The only time I went down was when
a machine was easing at the wrong time.

878
01:07:03,853 --> 01:07:06,914
-A little caution--
-Can get you killed, like reckless driving.

879
01:07:07,123 --> 01:07:08,955
-Cooper, it's too fast!
-I got this.

880
01:07:14,130 --> 01:07:17,794
-Should I disable the feedback?
-No. I need to feel the air.

881
01:07:22,105 --> 01:07:23,266
Here we go.

882
01:07:26,576 --> 01:07:27,873
It's just water!

883
01:07:29,379 --> 01:07:30,869
The stuff of life.

884
01:07:31,347 --> 01:07:32,678
Twelve hundred meters.

885
01:07:32,882 --> 01:07:35,044
-Do we have a fix on the beacon?
-Got it!

886
01:07:35,251 --> 01:07:37,720
-Can you maneuver?
-I'll need to shave some speed.

887
01:07:37,954 --> 01:07:40,685
I'm gonna spiral down on top of it.
Everybody hang on!

888
01:07:42,959 --> 01:07:44,017
Seven hundred.

889
01:07:46,162 --> 01:07:48,153
On my cue, CASE.

890
01:07:51,668 --> 01:07:53,158
Five hundred meters.

891
01:08:02,612 --> 01:08:03,670
Fire!

892
01:08:17,226 --> 01:08:18,250
Very graceful.

893
01:08:18,461 --> 01:08:19,553
No.

894
01:08:20,196 --> 01:08:22,255
But very efficient.

895
01:08:24,734 --> 01:08:26,896
What are you waiting for? Let's go!

896
01:08:27,170 --> 01:08:29,002
Go, go, go!

897
01:08:29,339 --> 01:08:32,673
Seven years per hour here.
Let's make it count!

898
01:08:58,801 --> 01:09:00,030
This way.

899
01:09:02,872 --> 01:09:04,636
About 200 meters.

900
01:09:14,150 --> 01:09:15,777
The gravity's punishing.

901
01:09:16,753 --> 01:09:18,812
Been floating through space too long?

902
01:09:19,022 --> 01:09:21,081
One hundred and thirty percent
Earth gravity.

903
01:09:21,424 --> 01:09:23,415
Come on. Come on.

904
01:09:26,029 --> 01:09:28,862
-There's nothing here.
-Should be right here.

905
01:09:30,800 --> 01:09:33,792
If the signal's coming from here, then...

906
01:09:43,413 --> 01:09:44,574
Her beacon.

907
01:09:52,722 --> 01:09:53,780
Wreckage.

908
01:09:54,424 --> 01:09:55,585
Where's the rest?

909
01:09:56,125 --> 01:09:57,615
Towards the mountains!

910
01:10:01,464 --> 01:10:03,296
Those aren't mountains.

911
01:10:04,967 --> 01:10:06,298
They're waves.

912
01:10:11,641 --> 01:10:14,303
Oh, shit. Oh, shit. Oh, shit.

913
01:10:14,777 --> 01:10:16,768
That one's moving away from us.

914
01:10:23,319 --> 01:10:24,980
We need the recorder.

915
01:10:35,164 --> 01:10:37,326
Brand, Doyle, back to the Ranger now!

916
01:10:37,533 --> 01:10:40,730
We're not leaving without her data.

917
01:10:40,970 --> 01:10:42,267
Get back here now!

918
01:10:42,472 --> 01:10:44,201
We do not have time!

919
01:10:44,407 --> 01:10:46,876
The second wave is coming!
We're in a swell.

920
01:10:48,244 --> 01:10:49,541
No, I got it.

921
01:10:49,812 --> 01:10:51,803
Get your ass back to the Ranger now!

922
01:10:56,819 --> 01:10:58,878
Brand, get back here now!

923
01:11:00,823 --> 01:11:02,985
No, Cooper, go. I can't make it.

924
01:11:03,459 --> 01:11:05,860
-Go!
-CASE, go get her.

925
01:11:10,633 --> 01:11:12,624
-Go!
-Get up, Brand!

926
01:11:13,569 --> 01:11:15,059
Go, go! Go!

927
01:11:16,639 --> 01:11:19,472
-I'm not gonna make it!
-Yes, you are. Yes, you are!

928
01:11:26,482 --> 01:11:28,644
CASE has her! Get back here, Doyle!

929
01:11:31,587 --> 01:11:32,611
Come on!

930
01:11:35,424 --> 01:11:36,892
Get to the hatch!

931
01:11:41,430 --> 01:11:42,920
Go, go! Go!

932
01:11:48,437 --> 01:11:49,529
Shit.

933
01:11:50,039 --> 01:11:51,871
Manually overriding inside hatch!

934
01:11:55,411 --> 01:11:56,572
Cooper! Wait!

935
01:12:03,920 --> 01:12:07,584
The engines are flooded!
I'm gonna have to shut her down.

936
01:12:40,990 --> 01:12:42,355
Holy shit.

937
01:12:46,796 --> 01:12:48,127
Hang on!

938
01:13:19,362 --> 01:13:20,659
CASE, what's the problem?

939
01:13:20,863 --> 01:13:23,195
Too waterlogged. Let it drain.

940
01:13:24,367 --> 01:13:25,493
Goddamn it!

941
01:13:25,701 --> 01:13:30,332
-I told you to leave me! Why didn't you?
-I told you to get your ass back here!

942
01:13:30,539 --> 01:13:32,439
One of us was thinking about
the mission!

943
01:13:32,608 --> 01:13:35,202
You were thinking about
getting home.

944
01:13:37,046 --> 01:13:39,344
I was trying to do the right thing!

945
01:13:39,548 --> 01:13:41,482
Can you tell that to Doyle?

946
01:13:43,753 --> 01:13:46,051
-CASE, how much time?
-Forty-five to an hour.

947
01:13:47,757 --> 01:13:49,088
The stuff of life, huh?

948
01:13:49,992 --> 01:13:52,120
What's this gonna cost us, Brand?

949
01:13:52,395 --> 01:13:54,386
A lot. Decades.

950
01:14:01,537 --> 01:14:02,868
What happened to Miller?

951
01:14:03,739 --> 01:14:06,299
Judging by the wreckage, she was...

952
01:14:06,575 --> 01:14:09,135
...broken up by
a wave soon after impact.

953
01:14:09,412 --> 01:14:12,143
How's the wreckage stayed together
after all these years?

954
01:14:13,416 --> 01:14:15,407
Because of the time slippage.

955
01:14:16,519 --> 01:14:21,013
On this planet's time,
she just landed hours ago.

956
01:14:21,958 --> 01:14:24,222
She probably just died minutes ago.

957
01:14:24,527 --> 01:14:28,521
The data Doyle received was just
the initial status echoing endlessly.

958
01:14:28,931 --> 01:14:31,093
Oh, we are not prepared for this.

959
01:14:33,469 --> 01:14:36,803
You eggheads have the survival skills
of a Boy Scout troop.

960
01:14:37,106 --> 01:14:40,406
Well, we got this far on our brains.
Farther than any human in history.

961
01:14:40,609 --> 01:14:42,099
Well, not far enough!

962
01:14:42,445 --> 01:14:45,938
And now we're stuck here till
there won't be anyone on Earth to save.

963
01:14:48,985 --> 01:14:51,647
I'm counting every minute,
same as you, Cooper.

964
01:14:56,459 --> 01:14:58,120
Is there any possibility...

965
01:14:58,461 --> 01:15:02,125
...some kind of way
we can all jump in a black hole?

966
01:15:02,365 --> 01:15:04,060
Gain back the years?

967
01:15:04,600 --> 01:15:06,159
Don't shake your head at me.

968
01:15:06,469 --> 01:15:08,130
Time is relative, okay?

969
01:15:08,471 --> 01:15:11,463
It can stretch and it can squeeze, but...

970
01:15:11,974 --> 01:15:14,136
...it can't run backwards. It just can't.

971
01:15:14,343 --> 01:15:18,780
The only thing that can move across
dimensions, like time, is gravity.

972
01:15:21,484 --> 01:15:22,645
Okay.

973
01:15:24,587 --> 01:15:28,319
The beings that led us here,
they communicate...

974
01:15:28,657 --> 01:15:30,455
-...through gravity, right?
-Yes.

975
01:15:30,659 --> 01:15:32,821
Could they be talking to us
from the future?

976
01:15:33,729 --> 01:15:35,993
-Maybe.
-Okay. if they can...

977
01:15:37,199 --> 01:15:40,464
"They" are beings of five dimensions.

978
01:15:40,870 --> 01:15:44,204
To them, time might be
another physical dimension.

979
01:15:44,540 --> 01:15:45,871
To them...

980
01:15:46,308 --> 01:15:50,438
...the past might be a canyon
that they can climb into and the future...

981
01:15:50,646 --> 01:15:53,638
...a mountain they can climb up.
But to us, it's not, okay?

982
01:15:54,717 --> 01:15:57,152
Look, Cooper, I screwed up. I'm sorry.

983
01:15:59,321 --> 01:16:01,153
But you knew about relativity.

984
01:16:04,260 --> 01:16:05,421
Brand...

985
01:16:10,332 --> 01:16:12,232
...my daughter was 10 years old.

986
01:16:12,868 --> 01:16:15,633
I couldn't teach her
Einstein's theories before I left.

987
01:16:16,072 --> 01:16:18,905
Couldn't you have told her
you were going to save the world?

988
01:16:19,508 --> 01:16:20,532
No.

989
01:16:22,211 --> 01:16:26,375
When you become a parent,
one thing becomes really clear.

990
01:16:27,883 --> 01:16:31,046
And that is that you want to make sure
your children feel safe.

991
01:16:32,088 --> 01:16:35,854
And it rules out telling a 10-year-old
that the world is ending.

992
01:16:38,294 --> 01:16:39,659
Cooper?

993
01:16:43,399 --> 01:16:45,697
-How long for the engines?
-A minute or two.

994
01:16:45,901 --> 01:16:48,233
We don't have it. Helmets on!

995
01:16:52,074 --> 01:16:53,769
Brand, co-pilot, you're up.

996
01:16:53,976 --> 01:16:56,468
CASE, blow cabin oxygen
through the main thrusters!

997
01:16:56,679 --> 01:16:58,408
-We're gonna spark it.
-Roger that.

998
01:16:58,714 --> 01:17:00,045
-Locked.
-Depressurizing.

999
01:17:05,921 --> 01:17:07,150
Engines up!

1000
01:17:36,018 --> 01:17:37,508
Hello, Rom.

1001
01:17:37,987 --> 01:17:39,648
I've waited years.

1002
01:17:40,723 --> 01:17:42,589
How many years?

1003
01:17:44,393 --> 01:17:45,656
By now it must be...

1004
01:17:45,828 --> 01:17:49,230
Twenty-three years, four months,
eight days.

1005
01:17:53,669 --> 01:17:54,693
Doyle?

1006
01:18:01,043 --> 01:18:04,035
I thought I was prepared.
I knew the theory.

1007
01:18:05,414 --> 01:18:06,904
Reality's different.

1008
01:18:07,983 --> 01:18:09,144
And Miller?

1009
01:18:10,719 --> 01:18:12,710
There's nothing here for us.

1010
01:18:15,491 --> 01:18:16,981
Why didn't you sleep?

1011
01:18:17,927 --> 01:18:22,023
I had a couple of stretches. But I
stopped believing you'd be back.

1012
01:18:22,531 --> 01:18:25,523
And something seemed wrong
about dreaming my life away.

1013
01:18:26,202 --> 01:18:29,194
I learned what I could
from the black hole...

1014
01:18:29,572 --> 01:18:32,007
...but I couldn't send anything
to your father.

1015
01:18:32,208 --> 01:18:34,370
We've been receiving,
but nothing gets out.

1016
01:18:34,643 --> 01:18:36,702
-Is he alive?
-Oh, yeah.

1017
01:18:37,112 --> 01:18:38,910
-Yeah?
-Yeah.

1018
01:18:41,917 --> 01:18:44,909
We've got years of messages stored.

1019
01:18:45,221 --> 01:18:46,245
Cooper.

1020
01:18:46,555 --> 01:18:49,547
Messages span 23 years.

1021
01:18:51,594 --> 01:18:53,255
Play it from the beginning.

1022
01:18:56,298 --> 01:18:58,164
Hey, Dad.

1023
01:18:58,867 --> 01:19:01,268
Checking in. Saying hi.

1024
01:19:04,073 --> 01:19:05,802
Finished second in school.

1025
01:19:07,409 --> 01:19:09,810
Miss Kurling's still giving me C's though.

1026
01:19:10,112 --> 01:19:12,945
Pulled me down, but second's not bad.

1027
01:19:14,116 --> 01:19:16,278
Grandpa attended the ceremony.

1028
01:19:20,889 --> 01:19:22,789
I met another girl, Dad.

1029
01:19:25,327 --> 01:19:27,318
I really think this is the one.

1030
01:19:28,631 --> 01:19:31,225
Name's Lois. That's her right there.

1031
01:19:34,270 --> 01:19:36,261
Murphy stole Grandpa's car.

1032
01:19:36,939 --> 01:19:39,601
She crashed it. She's okay, though.

1033
01:19:40,276 --> 01:19:41,334
Hey, Dad.

1034
01:19:42,278 --> 01:19:43,609
Look at this!

1035
01:19:44,446 --> 01:19:45,936
You're a grandpa.

1036
01:19:47,349 --> 01:19:49,010
His name's Jesse.

1037
01:19:49,718 --> 01:19:54,280
I wanted to call him Coop,
but Lois says maybe next time.

1038
01:19:54,957 --> 01:19:59,360
Donald says he's already earned the
"great" part, so we just leave it at that.

1039
01:20:02,564 --> 01:20:04,362
Oh, dear. Oh, dear.

1040
01:20:05,134 --> 01:20:06,761
Say "Bye-bye, Grandpa."

1041
01:20:07,136 --> 01:20:08,968
Bye-bye, Grandpa. Okay.

1042
01:20:09,371 --> 01:20:11,863
Sorry it's been a while.

1043
01:20:13,275 --> 01:20:14,299
Just...

1044
01:20:16,512 --> 01:20:18,503
...what with Jesse and all.

1045
01:20:25,020 --> 01:20:27,352
Grandpa died last week.

1046
01:20:27,790 --> 01:20:30,851
We buried him out in the back forty
next to Mom and...

1047
01:20:31,760 --> 01:20:32,818
...Jesse.

1048
01:20:33,696 --> 01:20:36,791
Which is where we would have
buried you if you'd ever...

1049
01:20:37,533 --> 01:20:39,023
...come back.

1050
01:20:40,536 --> 01:20:42,800
Murph was there at the funeral.

1051
01:20:43,405 --> 01:20:46,170
We don't see her that much,
but she came for that.

1052
01:20:49,378 --> 01:20:51,813
You aren't listening to this, I know that.

1053
01:20:54,917 --> 01:20:57,682
All these messages are just, like...

1054
01:20:58,854 --> 01:21:02,518
...drifting out there in the darkness.

1055
01:21:03,592 --> 01:21:05,924
Lois says that...

1056
01:21:06,729 --> 01:21:09,494
...I have to let you go.

1057
01:21:09,865 --> 01:21:11,697
And...

1058
01:21:12,167 --> 01:21:13,191
...so...

1059
01:21:16,538 --> 01:21:19,599
...I guess I'm letting you go.

1060
01:21:20,576 --> 01:21:23,238
I don't know where you are, Dad...

1061
01:21:23,579 --> 01:21:26,514
...but I hope that you're at peace.

1062
01:21:28,317 --> 01:21:29,375
Goodbye.

1063
01:21:42,965 --> 01:21:44,228
Hey, Dad.

1064
01:21:47,069 --> 01:21:48,127
Hey, Murph.

1065
01:21:48,404 --> 01:21:50,338
You son of a bitch.

1066
01:21:54,042 --> 01:21:56,511
I never made one of these
when you were responding...

1067
01:21:56,712 --> 01:21:59,044
...because I was so mad
at you for leaving.

1068
01:22:02,084 --> 01:22:04,143
And then when you went quiet, it...

1069
01:22:06,789 --> 01:22:09,622
...seemed like I should live with
that decision. And I have.

1070
01:22:14,129 --> 01:22:16,564
But today's my birthday.

1071
01:22:20,235 --> 01:22:23,398
And it's a special one,
because you told me...

1072
01:22:27,509 --> 01:22:31,878
You once told me that when you
came back, we might be the same age.

1073
01:22:33,582 --> 01:22:36,643
And today I'm the age you were
when you left.

1074
01:22:39,655 --> 01:22:42,647
So it would be a real good time
for you to come back.

1075
01:22:56,205 --> 01:23:00,802
I didn't mean to intrude. It's just that
I've never seen you in here before.

1076
01:23:01,143 --> 01:23:03,305
I've never been in here before.

1077
01:23:05,180 --> 01:23:08,172
I talk to Amelia all the time.

1078
01:23:08,684 --> 01:23:10,015
It helps.

1079
01:23:10,686 --> 01:23:13,280
-I'm glad you've started.
-I haven't.

1080
01:23:13,622 --> 01:23:16,114
I just had something I needed to get out.

1081
01:23:16,458 --> 01:23:18,790
-I know they're still out there.
-I know.

1082
01:23:19,061 --> 01:23:20,222
There are so many reasons...

1083
01:23:20,395 --> 01:23:23,160
...their communications might not be
getting through.

1084
01:23:23,565 --> 01:23:24,726
I know, professor.

1085
01:23:25,467 --> 01:23:28,300
I'm not sure what I'm more afraid of.

1086
01:23:29,538 --> 01:23:31,870
Them never coming back or...

1087
01:23:33,208 --> 01:23:36,200
...coming back to find we've failed.

1088
01:23:36,478 --> 01:23:37,877
Then let's succeed.

1089
01:23:38,947 --> 01:23:39,971
So...

1090
01:23:40,682 --> 01:23:44,676
...back to the fourth iteration.
Let's run it through some new fields.

1091
01:23:45,020 --> 01:23:48,183
With respect, professor,
we've tried that hundreds of times.

1092
01:23:48,524 --> 01:23:52,188
It only has to work once, Murph.

1093
01:23:55,497 --> 01:23:59,331
Every rivet that they strike
could have been a bullet.

1094
01:23:59,535 --> 01:24:01,503
We've done well for the world here...

1095
01:24:01,703 --> 01:24:06,539
...whether or not we crack the equation
before I kick the bucket.

1096
01:24:06,742 --> 01:24:09,507
-Don't be morbid, professor.
-I'm not afraid of death.

1097
01:24:10,245 --> 01:24:12,077
I'm an old physicist.

1098
01:24:13,348 --> 01:24:15,009
I'm afraid of time.

1099
01:24:22,324 --> 01:24:23,348
Time.

1100
01:24:25,394 --> 01:24:27,055
You're afraid of time.

1101
01:24:29,665 --> 01:24:31,633
We've been trying to solve
the equation...

1102
01:24:31,833 --> 01:24:34,268
...without changing
the assumption about time.

1103
01:24:34,469 --> 01:24:36,130
-And?
-And that means...

1104
01:24:36,338 --> 01:24:40,741
...each iteration is an attempt
to prove its own proof.

1105
01:24:41,109 --> 01:24:43,100
It's recursive. It's nonsensical.

1106
01:24:43,412 --> 01:24:45,380
Are you calling my life's work...

1107
01:24:45,581 --> 01:24:47,071
...nonsense, Murph?

1108
01:24:47,382 --> 01:24:49,350
No, I'm saying you've been trying to...

1109
01:24:49,551 --> 01:24:53,385
...finish it with one arm-- No, with
both arms. --tied behind your back.

1110
01:24:55,457 --> 01:24:57,448
And I don't understand why.

1111
01:24:58,427 --> 01:25:00,259
I'm an old man, Murph.

1112
01:25:01,196 --> 01:25:03,756
Can we take this point up
at another time?

1113
01:25:03,999 --> 01:25:06,093
I want to talk to my daughter.

1114
01:25:08,270 --> 01:25:10,705
Stepping out into the universe...

1115
01:25:10,939 --> 01:25:14,705
...we must confront the reality
of interstellar travel.

1116
01:25:15,611 --> 01:25:20,105
We must reach far beyond
our own lifespans.

1117
01:25:20,449 --> 01:25:25,444
We must think not as individuals
but as a species.

1118
01:25:28,290 --> 01:25:30,918
Do not go gentle into that good night

1119
01:25:33,128 --> 01:25:35,756
TARS kept the Endurance
right where we needed her.

1120
01:25:35,964 --> 01:25:38,626
But the trip took years longer
than we anticipated.

1121
01:25:38,967 --> 01:25:42,460
We no longer have the fuel
to visit both prospects, so...

1122
01:25:42,804 --> 01:25:44,294
...we have to choose.

1123
01:25:45,507 --> 01:25:46,599
But how?

1124
01:25:47,676 --> 01:25:49,337
They're both promising.

1125
01:25:50,879 --> 01:25:55,316
Edmunds' data is better, but Dr. Mann
is the one still transmitting.

1126
01:25:55,651 --> 01:25:58,484
We've no reason to suspect
Edmunds's data would have soured.

1127
01:25:59,388 --> 01:26:01,880
His world has key elements
to sustain human life.

1128
01:26:02,090 --> 01:26:05,253
-As does Dr. Mann's.
-Cooper, this is my field.

1129
01:26:06,395 --> 01:26:07,419
And...

1130
01:26:08,263 --> 01:26:10,357
...I believe Edmunds'
is the better prospect.

1131
01:26:10,565 --> 01:26:13,899
-Why?
-Gargantua, that's why.

1132
01:26:15,037 --> 01:26:19,031
Look at Miller's planet.
Hydrocarbons, organics, yes...

1133
01:26:19,307 --> 01:26:23,676
...but no life. Sterile.
We'll find the same thing on Mann's.

1134
01:26:24,079 --> 01:26:25,740
Because of the black hole?

1135
01:26:26,048 --> 01:26:29,040
Murphy's Law.
Whatever can happen will happen.

1136
01:26:29,251 --> 01:26:31,549
Accident is the first building block
of evolution.

1137
01:26:31,753 --> 01:26:34,586
But when you're orbiting a black hole
not enough can happen.

1138
01:26:34,790 --> 01:26:39,057
It sucks in asteroids and comets, events
which would otherwise reach you.

1139
01:26:39,461 --> 01:26:41,293
We need to go further afield.

1140
01:26:41,897 --> 01:26:44,229
You once said that Dr. Mann...

1141
01:26:45,500 --> 01:26:46,990
...was the best of us.

1142
01:26:47,302 --> 01:26:50,465
He's remarkable.
We're only here because of him.

1143
01:26:51,006 --> 01:26:52,804
And yet here he is.

1144
01:26:53,008 --> 01:26:57,809
He's on the ground, and he's sending an
unambiguous message, telling us to...

1145
01:26:58,013 --> 01:26:59,811
...come to his planet.

1146
01:27:00,015 --> 01:27:04,612
Granted, but Edmunds' data
is more promising.

1147
01:27:05,087 --> 01:27:06,748
-We should vote.
-If we vote...

1148
01:27:06,922 --> 01:27:09,414
...there's something
you should know.

1149
01:27:09,624 --> 01:27:10,648
Brand?

1150
01:27:10,859 --> 01:27:12,418
He has a right to know.

1151
01:27:12,994 --> 01:27:14,655
That has nothing to do with it.

1152
01:27:14,963 --> 01:27:17,796
-What does?
-She's in love with Wolf Edmunds.

1153
01:27:18,133 --> 01:27:20,625
-Is that true?
-Yes.

1154
01:27:23,805 --> 01:27:26,536
And that makes me want to
follow my heart.

1155
01:27:29,978 --> 01:27:33,642
Maybe we've spent too long
trying to figure all this out with theory.

1156
01:27:33,849 --> 01:27:35,317
You're a scientist, Brand.

1157
01:27:35,517 --> 01:27:36,951
So listen to me...

1158
01:27:37,786 --> 01:27:42,519
...when I say that love
isn't something we invented. It's...

1159
01:27:43,492 --> 01:27:45,824
...observable, powerful.

1160
01:27:47,929 --> 01:27:49,556
It has to mean something.

1161
01:27:49,831 --> 01:27:53,927
Love has meaning, yes. Social utility,
social bonding, child rearing...

1162
01:27:54,136 --> 01:27:57,868
We love people who have died.
Where's the social utility in that?

1163
01:27:58,507 --> 01:27:59,531
None.

1164
01:27:59,741 --> 01:28:03,439
Maybe it means something more,
something we can't...

1165
01:28:03,678 --> 01:28:05,669
...yet understand.

1166
01:28:05,981 --> 01:28:09,315
Maybe it's some evidence, some...

1167
01:28:10,485 --> 01:28:14,718
...artifact of a higher dimension
that we can't consciously perceive.

1168
01:28:15,056 --> 01:28:19,653
I'm drawn across the universe
to someone I haven't seen in a decade...

1169
01:28:19,995 --> 01:28:22,726
...who I know is probably dead.

1170
01:28:23,064 --> 01:28:26,329
Love is the one thing
we're capable of perceiving...

1171
01:28:26,535 --> 01:28:29,368
...that transcends dimensions
of time and space.

1172
01:28:31,406 --> 01:28:35,570
Maybe we should trust that,
even if we can't understand it yet.

1173
01:28:40,749 --> 01:28:42,239
All right, Cooper...

1174
01:28:44,219 --> 01:28:45,380
...yes...

1175
01:28:46,721 --> 01:28:50,055
...the tiniest possibility
of seeing Wolf again excites me.

1176
01:28:50,725 --> 01:28:52,386
That doesn't mean I'm wrong.

1177
01:28:53,295 --> 01:28:55,286
Honestly, Amelia...

1178
01:28:57,098 --> 01:28:58,259
...it might.

1179
01:29:05,106 --> 01:29:07,871
TARS, chart a course for Dr. Mann's.

1180
01:29:24,826 --> 01:29:27,818
We'll lose about a third again.
But next year...

1181
01:29:30,131 --> 01:29:32,964
...I'm gonna work Nelson's farm
and we'll make it up.

1182
01:29:33,935 --> 01:29:35,596
What happened to Nelson?

1183
01:29:41,543 --> 01:29:43,944
Murph, would you like
some more soufflé?

1184
01:29:44,613 --> 01:29:47,275
I'm full, thanks. It was delicious.

1185
01:29:48,183 --> 01:29:50,174
Coop, finish your fritter, please.

1186
01:29:52,354 --> 01:29:56,018
Will you spend the night? Your room
is exactly as you left it. It's ready.

1187
01:29:56,224 --> 01:29:57,692
I need to get back.

1188
01:29:57,926 --> 01:30:00,293
My sewing machine is in there,
but there's plenty--

1189
01:30:00,495 --> 01:30:01,758
I need to...

1190
01:30:04,633 --> 01:30:06,192
Too many memories.

1191
01:30:06,801 --> 01:30:09,600
We might have something for that.
Hey, Coop.

1192
01:30:17,145 --> 01:30:18,169
The dust.

1193
01:30:21,049 --> 01:30:23,313
Lois, I have a friend who...

1194
01:30:23,551 --> 01:30:25,315
...could look at his lungs.

1195
01:30:43,571 --> 01:30:46,131
-Amelia, I'm sorry.
-You were just being objective.

1196
01:30:46,341 --> 01:30:49,743
Unless you're punishing me
for screwing up on Miller's planet.

1197
01:30:49,945 --> 01:30:52,243
No, this wasn't a personal decision.

1198
01:30:53,081 --> 01:30:56,949
If you're wrong, you will have
a very personal decision to make.

1199
01:30:57,185 --> 01:31:00,246
Your fuel calculations
are based on a return journey.

1200
01:31:00,588 --> 01:31:04,456
Strike out on Mann's planet, and we'll
have to decide whether to return home...

1201
01:31:04,759 --> 01:31:06,853
...or push on to Edmunds' with Plan B.

1202
01:31:07,228 --> 01:31:10,892
Starting a colony could save us
from extinction.

1203
01:31:11,199 --> 01:31:14,328
You might have to decide
between seeing your children again...

1204
01:31:14,536 --> 01:31:16,368
...and the future of the human race.

1205
01:31:17,906 --> 01:31:19,601
I trust you'll be as objective then.

1206
01:31:26,481 --> 01:31:28,472
He's been asking for you
since he came to.

1207
01:31:28,683 --> 01:31:30,173
We were trying to reach you.

1208
01:31:36,157 --> 01:31:37,181
Murph.

1209
01:31:38,193 --> 01:31:39,683
I'm here, professor.

1210
01:31:40,395 --> 01:31:42,727
I let you all down.

1211
01:31:43,064 --> 01:31:46,398
No, you got us so far. Real close.

1212
01:31:48,269 --> 01:31:50,033
I'll finish what you started.

1213
01:31:50,238 --> 01:31:52,400
Good, good Murph.

1214
01:31:54,843 --> 01:31:57,005
You had faith...

1215
01:31:58,346 --> 01:31:59,507
...all those...

1216
01:32:00,648 --> 01:32:02,275
...all those years.

1217
01:32:04,019 --> 01:32:05,043
I...

1218
01:32:05,520 --> 01:32:07,852
I asked you to have faith.

1219
01:32:09,724 --> 01:32:12,386
I wanted you to believe...

1220
01:32:14,062 --> 01:32:15,791
...that your father...

1221
01:32:18,900 --> 01:32:20,561
...would come back.

1222
01:32:20,802 --> 01:32:22,065
I do, professor.

1223
01:32:23,705 --> 01:32:25,195
Forgive me, Murph.

1224
01:32:25,940 --> 01:32:27,931
There's nothing to forgive.

1225
01:32:30,645 --> 01:32:31,669
I...

1226
01:32:32,247 --> 01:32:34,079
I lied, Murph.

1227
01:32:36,484 --> 01:32:38,248
I lied to you.

1228
01:32:44,592 --> 01:32:47,823
There was no need for him to...

1229
01:32:49,130 --> 01:32:50,894
To come back.

1230
01:32:53,501 --> 01:32:56,835
There is no way to help us.

1231
01:32:58,106 --> 01:32:59,130
But Plan A...

1232
01:32:59,707 --> 01:33:02,836
All this. All these people.

1233
01:33:03,945 --> 01:33:05,743
And the equation.

1234
01:33:09,918 --> 01:33:11,579
Did my father know?

1235
01:33:15,356 --> 01:33:17,017
Did he leave me?

1236
01:33:20,962 --> 01:33:21,986
Do...

1237
01:33:23,431 --> 01:33:24,523
...not...

1238
01:33:25,633 --> 01:33:26,725
...go...

1239
01:33:28,036 --> 01:33:29,526
...gentle...

1240
01:33:31,639 --> 01:33:32,663
No.

1241
01:33:34,342 --> 01:33:35,366
No!

1242
01:33:36,644 --> 01:33:38,305
You can't leave.

1243
01:33:38,680 --> 01:33:39,943
You-- No.

1244
01:33:41,149 --> 01:33:45,814
Dr. Brand, I'm sorry to tell you that
your father died today. He had no pain.

1245
01:33:46,287 --> 01:33:47,948
He was at peace.

1246
01:33:48,623 --> 01:33:50,682
I'm very sorry for your loss.

1247
01:34:03,304 --> 01:34:04,965
Brand, did you know?

1248
01:34:07,375 --> 01:34:09,036
He told you, right?

1249
01:34:12,147 --> 01:34:13,308
You knew.

1250
01:34:15,350 --> 01:34:17,011
This was all a sham.

1251
01:34:21,523 --> 01:34:23,855
You left us here.

1252
01:34:25,226 --> 01:34:26,716
To suffocate.

1253
01:34:28,897 --> 01:34:30,228
To starve.

1254
01:34:57,625 --> 01:34:58,956
Frozen cloud.

1255
01:37:23,371 --> 01:37:24,702
It's okay.

1256
01:37:24,972 --> 01:37:26,462
It's okay.

1257
01:37:33,181 --> 01:37:34,671
It's okay.

1258
01:37:40,221 --> 01:37:42,087
Pray you never learn...

1259
01:37:43,391 --> 01:37:46,588
...just how good it can be
to see another face.

1260
01:37:55,203 --> 01:37:57,535
I hadn't a lot of hope to begin with, but...

1261
01:37:57,872 --> 01:38:00,034
...after so long, I had none.

1262
01:38:01,709 --> 01:38:04,542
My supplies were completely exhausted.

1263
01:38:06,748 --> 01:38:10,241
The last time I went to sleep,
I didn't even set a waking date.

1264
01:38:14,222 --> 01:38:16,884
You have literally raised me
from the dead.

1265
01:38:17,658 --> 01:38:18,682
Lazarus.

1266
01:38:20,995 --> 01:38:24,226
-What about the others?
-I'm afraid you're it, sir.

1267
01:38:24,932 --> 01:38:27,367
So far, surely.

1268
01:38:28,136 --> 01:38:32,869
No, our present situation is that there's
very little chance of rescuing any others.

1269
01:38:36,711 --> 01:38:38,702
Dr. Mann. Dr. Mann?

1270
01:38:39,781 --> 01:38:41,442
Tell us about your world.

1271
01:38:44,952 --> 01:38:46,784
Our world, we hope.

1272
01:38:50,658 --> 01:38:52,786
Our world is cold...

1273
01:38:53,261 --> 01:38:54,422
...stark...

1274
01:38:56,297 --> 01:38:58,163
...but undeniably beautiful.

1275
01:39:00,401 --> 01:39:03,666
The days are 67 hours long, cold.

1276
01:39:07,675 --> 01:39:09,040
The nights are...

1277
01:39:09,277 --> 01:39:11,609
...67 far colder hours.

1278
01:39:17,452 --> 01:39:21,685
The gravity is a very, very pleasant
80 percent of the Earth's.

1279
01:39:21,956 --> 01:39:24,948
Now, up here where I landed,
the water is alkali...

1280
01:39:25,293 --> 01:39:28,957
...and the air has too much ammonia
to breathe for more than a few minutes...

1281
01:39:29,330 --> 01:39:33,494
...but down at the surface,
and there is a surface...

1282
01:39:33,868 --> 01:39:35,563
...the chlorine dissipates.

1283
01:39:35,870 --> 01:39:39,636
The ammonia gives way to crystalline
hydrocarbons and breathable air.

1284
01:39:39,974 --> 01:39:41,464
To organics.

1285
01:39:41,809 --> 01:39:44,141
Possibly even to life.

1286
01:39:44,979 --> 01:39:46,879
We might be sharing this world.

1287
01:39:47,448 --> 01:39:49,348
These readings are from the surface?

1288
01:39:49,584 --> 01:39:52,713
Over the years,
I've dropped various probes.

1289
01:39:53,087 --> 01:39:56,455
-How far have you explored?
-I've mounted several major expeditions.

1290
01:39:56,657 --> 01:40:00,594
But with oxygen in limited supply, KIPP
there really did most of the legwork.

1291
01:40:00,828 --> 01:40:03,490
-What went wrong with him, sir?
-Degeneration.

1292
01:40:03,731 --> 01:40:07,224
He misidentified the first organics
we found as ammonia crystals.

1293
01:40:07,502 --> 01:40:11,405
We struggled on for a time, but
ultimately I decommissioned him and...

1294
01:40:11,606 --> 01:40:14,098
...used his power source
to keep the mission going.

1295
01:40:14,342 --> 01:40:16,743
I thought I was alone
before I shut him down.

1296
01:40:17,011 --> 01:40:18,843
Would you like me to look at him?

1297
01:40:19,413 --> 01:40:21,745
No. No. He needs a human touch.

1298
01:40:22,116 --> 01:40:26,178
Dr. Brand, CASE is relaying a message
for you from the comm station.

1299
01:40:26,554 --> 01:40:28,989
Okay. Be right there. Excuse me.

1300
01:40:40,902 --> 01:40:43,496
Dr. Brand, I'm sorry to tell you
that your father died.

1301
01:40:44,338 --> 01:40:45,828
He had no pain.

1302
01:40:48,943 --> 01:40:50,433
He was at peace.

1303
01:40:52,647 --> 01:40:54,376
I'm sorry for your loss.

1304
01:40:56,884 --> 01:40:57,942
Is that Murph?

1305
01:40:58,519 --> 01:41:00,851
She's... She's grown.

1306
01:41:01,155 --> 01:41:02,714
Brand, did you know?

1307
01:41:03,891 --> 01:41:05,552
He told you, right?

1308
01:41:08,729 --> 01:41:09,890
You knew.

1309
01:41:11,666 --> 01:41:13,156
This was all a sham.

1310
01:41:14,201 --> 01:41:15,862
You left us here.

1311
01:41:17,972 --> 01:41:19,462
To suffocate.

1312
01:41:21,609 --> 01:41:22,940
To starve.

1313
01:41:26,280 --> 01:41:28,044
Did my father know too?

1314
01:41:30,585 --> 01:41:31,916
Dad?

1315
01:41:33,754 --> 01:41:35,586
I just want to know...

1316
01:41:36,991 --> 01:41:39,085
...if you left me here to die.

1317
01:41:41,495 --> 01:41:43,486
I just have to know.

1318
01:41:49,036 --> 01:41:50,435
Cooper, my...

1319
01:41:51,939 --> 01:41:53,873
My father dedicated...

1320
01:41:54,241 --> 01:41:59,236
...his whole life to Plan A.
I have no idea what she's talking about.

1321
01:42:00,948 --> 01:42:02,109
I do.

1322
01:42:07,254 --> 01:42:09,916
He never even hoped
to get the people off the Earth?

1323
01:42:11,125 --> 01:42:12,149
No.

1324
01:42:16,397 --> 01:42:20,129
But he's been trying to solve
the gravity equation for 40 years.

1325
01:42:20,735 --> 01:42:24,137
Amelia, your father solved his equation
before I even left.

1326
01:42:24,739 --> 01:42:26,468
Then why wouldn't he use it?

1327
01:42:26,674 --> 01:42:30,611
It couldn't reconcile relativity with
quantum mechanics. You need more.

1328
01:42:30,911 --> 01:42:32,743
More? More what?

1329
01:42:33,014 --> 01:42:35,915
More data.
You need to see into a black hole.

1330
01:42:36,117 --> 01:42:38,176
Laws of nature prohibit
a naked singularity.

1331
01:42:38,386 --> 01:42:39,751
Romilly, is that true?

1332
01:42:41,188 --> 01:42:44,852
If a black hole is an oyster,
then the singularity's the pearl inside.

1333
01:42:45,092 --> 01:42:47,754
The gravity's so strong,
it's always hidden in darkness...

1334
01:42:47,962 --> 01:42:49,396
...behind the horizon.

1335
01:42:50,831 --> 01:42:52,822
That's why we call it a black hole.

1336
01:42:53,100 --> 01:42:56,502
Okay, but if we see
beyond the horizon...

1337
01:42:57,104 --> 01:42:58,594
We can't, Coop.

1338
01:42:58,873 --> 01:43:01,205
Some things aren't meant to be known.

1339
01:43:01,609 --> 01:43:05,603
Your father had to find another way
to save the human race from extinction.

1340
01:43:05,813 --> 01:43:07,281
Plan B. A colony.

1341
01:43:07,481 --> 01:43:09,142
Why not tell people?

1342
01:43:10,718 --> 01:43:13,779
-Why keep building those stations?
-He knew how hard it would be...

1343
01:43:13,988 --> 01:43:17,947
...to get people to work together to save
the species instead of themselves.

1344
01:43:18,259 --> 01:43:19,351
Or their children.

1345
01:43:19,560 --> 01:43:20,584
Bullshit.

1346
01:43:20,795 --> 01:43:23,560
You never would've come unless
you believed you would save them.

1347
01:43:23,764 --> 01:43:26,893
Evolution has yet to transcend
that simple barrier.

1348
01:43:27,101 --> 01:43:29,502
We can care deeply, selflessly about...

1349
01:43:29,704 --> 01:43:33,265
...those we know, but that empathy
rarely extends beyond our line of sight.

1350
01:43:33,474 --> 01:43:35,169
But the lie.

1351
01:43:38,446 --> 01:43:40,881
That monstrous lie?

1352
01:43:42,116 --> 01:43:43,311
Unforgivable.

1353
01:43:43,551 --> 01:43:45,041
And he knew that.

1354
01:43:45,886 --> 01:43:50,050
He was prepared to destroy his own
humanity in order to save the species.

1355
01:43:50,324 --> 01:43:52,725
-He made an incredible sacrifice.
-No.

1356
01:43:54,195 --> 01:43:59,031
An incredible sacrifice is being made by
the people on Earth who are gonna die!

1357
01:43:59,467 --> 01:44:01,629
Because in his fucking arrogance...

1358
01:44:02,069 --> 01:44:04,128
...he declared their case hopeless.

1359
01:44:04,839 --> 01:44:06,329
I'm sorry, Cooper...

1360
01:44:06,874 --> 01:44:08,364
...their case is hopeless.

1361
01:44:08,576 --> 01:44:10,408
No. No.

1362
01:44:11,245 --> 01:44:12,735
We are the future.

1363
01:44:16,417 --> 01:44:18,351
Cooper, what can I do?

1364
01:44:21,555 --> 01:44:23,387
Let me go home.

1365
01:44:23,958 --> 01:44:25,892
You're absolutely positive?

1366
01:44:26,494 --> 01:44:28,986
His solution was correct.
He'd had it for years.

1367
01:44:29,663 --> 01:44:32,291
-It's worthless?
-It's half the answer.

1368
01:44:33,267 --> 01:44:36,259
Okay, how do you find the other half?

1369
01:44:36,837 --> 01:44:38,464
Out there? A black hole.

1370
01:44:38,672 --> 01:44:41,505
-But stuck down here on Earth?
-Yeah?

1371
01:44:42,843 --> 01:44:44,504
I'm not sure you can.

1372
01:44:46,447 --> 01:44:49,109
God, they just pack up and leave.

1373
01:44:50,551 --> 01:44:52,610
What are they hoping to find?

1374
01:44:53,187 --> 01:44:54,211
Survival.

1375
01:45:02,029 --> 01:45:03,121
Damn it.

1376
01:45:06,033 --> 01:45:09,833
Murph, don't people
have a right to know?

1377
01:45:10,471 --> 01:45:14,499
Panic won't help. We just have to
keep working, same as ever.

1378
01:45:14,708 --> 01:45:18,008
But isn't that exactly what Professor
Brand was manipulating us to do?

1379
01:45:18,212 --> 01:45:21,477
Brand gave up on us.
I'm still trying to solve this.

1380
01:45:22,116 --> 01:45:23,174
So...

1381
01:45:24,618 --> 01:45:26,279
...do you have an idea?

1382
01:45:26,987 --> 01:45:28,318
A feeling.

1383
01:45:31,725 --> 01:45:33,716
I told you about my ghost.

1384
01:45:35,763 --> 01:45:37,925
My dad thought I called it a ghost...

1385
01:45:38,265 --> 01:45:39,892
...because I was scared of it.

1386
01:45:43,370 --> 01:45:44,701
But I was never scared of it.

1387
01:45:46,941 --> 01:45:49,569
I called it a ghost because it felt...

1388
01:45:52,012 --> 01:45:53,673
It felt like a person.

1389
01:45:56,217 --> 01:45:57,878
It was trying to tell me something.

1390
01:46:00,855 --> 01:46:03,847
If there's an answer here on Earth,
it's back there...

1391
01:46:04,325 --> 01:46:06,225
...somehow, in that room.

1392
01:46:06,760 --> 01:46:08,524
So I have to find it.

1393
01:46:11,966 --> 01:46:13,695
We're running out of time.

1394
01:46:15,603 --> 01:46:17,571
What about auxiliary oxygen scrubbers?

1395
01:46:17,771 --> 01:46:19,933
No, they can stay.
I'll be sleeping anyway.

1396
01:46:20,140 --> 01:46:21,630
-Hey, Coop?
-Yeah?

1397
01:46:21,842 --> 01:46:23,901
I have a suggestion
for your return journey.

1398
01:46:24,211 --> 01:46:26,771
-Yeah, what?
-Have one last crack at the black hole.

1399
01:46:28,682 --> 01:46:30,150
I'm going home, Rom.

1400
01:46:30,351 --> 01:46:33,685
Yeah, I know.
This isn't going to cost you any time.

1401
01:46:34,421 --> 01:46:36,253
There's a chance for people on Earth.

1402
01:46:37,024 --> 01:46:40,016
-Talk to me.
-Gargantua's an old spinning black hole.

1403
01:46:40,227 --> 01:46:44,061
-It's what we call a gentle singularity.
-Gentle?

1404
01:46:44,265 --> 01:46:47,701
They're hardly gentle.
But the tidal gravity is so quick that...

1405
01:46:47,902 --> 01:46:52,237
...something crossing the horizon fast
might survive. A probe, say.

1406
01:46:52,506 --> 01:46:56,909
-What happens after it crosses?
-After the horizon is a complete mystery.

1407
01:46:57,244 --> 01:47:00,214
So, what's to say
there isn't some way that the probe...

1408
01:47:00,414 --> 01:47:03,247
...can glimpse the singularity
and relay the quantum data?

1409
01:47:03,517 --> 01:47:06,714
If he's equipped to transmit
every form of energy that can pulse.

1410
01:47:06,921 --> 01:47:09,720
Just when did this probe
become a "he," professor?

1411
01:47:10,257 --> 01:47:12,021
TARS is the obvious candidate.

1412
01:47:12,826 --> 01:47:14,726
I've already told him what to look for.

1413
01:47:14,929 --> 01:47:17,626
I'd need the old optical transmitter
off KIPP, Cooper.

1414
01:47:17,831 --> 01:47:19,128
You'd do this for us?

1415
01:47:19,333 --> 01:47:23,236
Before you get all teary, remember that
as a robot I have to do anything you say.

1416
01:47:24,138 --> 01:47:25,606
Your cue light's broken.

1417
01:47:25,806 --> 01:47:27,296
I'm not joking.

1418
01:47:29,944 --> 01:47:33,175
I'll need TARS to remove and adapt
some components from KIPP.

1419
01:47:33,380 --> 01:47:35,678
I don't want to disturb
his archival functions.

1420
01:47:35,883 --> 01:47:37,373
I'll supervise.

1421
01:47:37,685 --> 01:47:38,709
All right.

1422
01:47:38,919 --> 01:47:41,445
Dr. Mann, we need to find
three secure sites.

1423
01:47:41,622 --> 01:47:43,556
One for Brand's lab, two for habitat.

1424
01:47:43,757 --> 01:47:46,158
Once those have landed
you don't want to move them.

1425
01:47:46,360 --> 01:47:48,886
I can take you to the probe sites,
but I don't think...

1426
01:47:49,096 --> 01:47:51,724
...these conditions will hold.
I think we should wait.

1427
01:47:51,932 --> 01:47:54,799
CASE is headed down with the rest
of the distillery equipment.

1428
01:47:55,002 --> 01:47:57,801
I'd really like to secure those sites
by nightfall.

1429
01:47:59,740 --> 01:48:02,801
Well, these squalls do usually blow over.

1430
01:48:03,243 --> 01:48:04,267
Okay, then.

1431
01:48:04,545 --> 01:48:06,707
-You'll need a long-range transmitter.
-Got it.

1432
01:48:06,947 --> 01:48:08,437
-Are you charged?
-Yeah.

1433
01:48:09,016 --> 01:48:10,040
Follow me.

1434
01:48:10,818 --> 01:48:12,912
TARS, 72 hours, yeah?

1435
01:48:13,187 --> 01:48:14,416
Roger that, Cooper.

1436
01:48:20,694 --> 01:48:23,720
Brand told me why you feel
you have to go back.

1437
01:48:25,566 --> 01:48:28,331
But I'd be remiss
if I didn't at least mention...

1438
01:48:28,669 --> 01:48:32,833
...that a mission such as ours
could certainly use an extra engineer.

1439
01:48:34,675 --> 01:48:38,908
You better slow down, turbo.
Safety first, CASE, remember.

1440
01:48:39,546 --> 01:48:41,310
Safety first, Cooper.

1441
01:48:42,549 --> 01:48:45,541
I have to tell you, Dr. Mann,
I'm honored to be a part of this.

1442
01:48:45,919 --> 01:48:50,914
Once we set up base camp and secure
those modules, my work's done here.

1443
01:48:51,358 --> 01:48:52,689
I'm going home.

1444
01:49:00,701 --> 01:49:04,194
You have attachments.
But even without a family...

1445
01:49:04,638 --> 01:49:09,075
...I can promise you that that yearning
to be with other people is powerful.

1446
01:49:09,743 --> 01:49:13,077
That emotion is at the foundation...

1447
01:49:13,747 --> 01:49:16,739
...of what makes us human.
It's not to be taken lightly.

1448
01:49:24,792 --> 01:49:27,318
-How long have you had that cough?
-A while.

1449
01:49:37,871 --> 01:49:39,862
Mom lets me play in here.

1450
01:49:41,608 --> 01:49:43,042
I don't touch your stuff.

1451
01:50:13,974 --> 01:50:15,635
Just take this gently.

1452
01:50:20,781 --> 01:50:24,149
You know why we couldn't just send
machines on these missions, don't you?

1453
01:50:24,785 --> 01:50:26,617
A machine doesn't improvise well...

1454
01:50:26,787 --> 01:50:29,415
...because you can't program
a fear of death.

1455
01:50:30,290 --> 01:50:32,520
Our survival instinct
is our single greatest...

1456
01:50:32,726 --> 01:50:36,321
...source of inspiration.
Take you, for example.

1457
01:50:37,030 --> 01:50:38,361
A father...

1458
01:50:38,899 --> 01:50:41,891
...with a survival instinct
that extends to your kids.

1459
01:50:43,070 --> 01:50:47,735
What does research tell us is the last
thing you'll see before you die?

1460
01:50:49,443 --> 01:50:51,878
Your children. Their faces.

1461
01:50:52,246 --> 01:50:54,647
At the moment of death,
your mind will push...

1462
01:50:54,848 --> 01:50:56,680
...a little bit harder to survive.

1463
01:50:57,384 --> 01:50:58,442
For them.

1464
01:50:59,286 --> 01:51:00,549
Deep breath.

1465
01:51:02,856 --> 01:51:06,622
I bet you're Coop.
Why don't you have a seat here for me?

1466
01:51:06,927 --> 01:51:09,259
It's bad. They cannot stay here.

1467
01:51:09,963 --> 01:51:11,226
-Okay?
-Yeah.

1468
01:51:19,540 --> 01:51:21,008
What's taking so long?

1469
01:51:21,208 --> 01:51:24,041
I am having trouble
completing the boot up.

1470
01:51:24,311 --> 01:51:25,801
I don't understand.

1471
01:51:32,619 --> 01:51:33,848
It's funny.

1472
01:51:35,923 --> 01:51:37,550
When I left Earth...

1473
01:51:38,559 --> 01:51:40,653
...I thought I was prepared to die.

1474
01:51:42,696 --> 01:51:44,027
The truth is...

1475
01:51:46,233 --> 01:51:48,861
...I never really considered
the possibility...

1476
01:51:49,036 --> 01:51:51,232
...that my planet wasn't the one.

1477
01:51:53,941 --> 01:51:56,239
Nothing worked out the way
it was supposed to.

1478
01:51:56,443 --> 01:51:57,501
Let's go.

1479
01:52:05,919 --> 01:52:08,684
All right, buddy,
give me a big deep breath.

1480
01:52:13,560 --> 01:52:14,994
What is this?

1481
01:52:15,395 --> 01:52:17,193
-What are you doing?
-I'm sorry!

1482
01:52:17,364 --> 01:52:19,230
I can't let you leave with that ship.

1483
01:52:19,566 --> 01:52:21,898
We'll need it to complete the mission...

1484
01:52:22,236 --> 01:52:25,638
...once the others realize
what this place isn't.

1485
01:52:25,939 --> 01:52:28,704
We cannot survive here. I'm sorry!

1486
01:52:29,142 --> 01:52:30,405
I'm sorry!

1487
01:52:39,920 --> 01:52:42,912
-They can't stay here anymore.
-You have to leave right now.

1488
01:52:43,223 --> 01:52:46,784
Let me make something abundantly
clear. You have a responsibility--

1489
01:52:46,960 --> 01:52:50,123
-Jesus!
-Coop, get her stuff. She's going home.

1490
01:52:50,430 --> 01:52:52,398
Dad didn't raise you
to be this dumb, Tom!

1491
01:52:52,599 --> 01:52:56,593
Dad didn't raise me, Grandpa did. He's
buried out back with Mom and Jesse.

1492
01:52:57,170 --> 01:52:59,832
You faked all the data?

1493
01:53:01,575 --> 01:53:02,633
Yeah.

1494
01:53:06,647 --> 01:53:08,274
There's no surface?

1495
01:53:08,482 --> 01:53:09,506
No.

1496
01:53:10,651 --> 01:53:12,585
I tried to do my duty...

1497
01:53:12,819 --> 01:53:17,814
...but I knew the day that I arrived here
that this place had nothing.

1498
01:53:18,458 --> 01:53:22,258
And I resisted the temptation for years...

1499
01:53:22,863 --> 01:53:24,957
...but I knew that...

1500
01:53:25,299 --> 01:53:28,132
...if I just pressed that button, then...

1501
01:53:28,435 --> 01:53:30,426
...somebody would come and save me.

1502
01:53:31,471 --> 01:53:32,961
You fucking coward.

1503
01:53:33,807 --> 01:53:34,831
Yes.

1504
01:53:40,280 --> 01:53:41,304
Yes.

1505
01:53:43,216 --> 01:53:44,240
Yes.

1506
01:53:45,519 --> 01:53:46,611
Yes.

1507
01:53:58,065 --> 01:54:02,627
Listen, if you're not going to go,
let your family go. Just save your family.

1508
01:54:03,136 --> 01:54:05,366
And we go live underground with you?

1509
01:54:05,572 --> 01:54:07,563
Pray that Daddy comes to save us?

1510
01:54:07,774 --> 01:54:11,176
Dad's not coming back.
He never was. It's up to me.

1511
01:54:11,478 --> 01:54:13,207
You're gonna save everybody?

1512
01:54:13,613 --> 01:54:16,275
-Because Dad couldn't do it.
-Dad didn't even try!

1513
01:54:16,616 --> 01:54:18,778
Dad just abandoned us!

1514
01:54:19,386 --> 01:54:21,320
He left us here to die.

1515
01:54:22,022 --> 01:54:23,683
Nobody's going with you.

1516
01:54:27,394 --> 01:54:30,091
You gonna wait for your next kid to die?

1517
01:54:33,100 --> 01:54:34,397
Get out.

1518
01:54:35,302 --> 01:54:36,792
And don't come back.

1519
01:54:41,508 --> 01:54:43,340
You can keep my stuff.

1520
01:54:54,254 --> 01:54:55,619
Stop it!

1521
01:54:58,692 --> 01:54:59,921
No. No!

1522
01:55:01,361 --> 01:55:04,194
Dr. Mann, there's a 50-50 chance
you're gonna kill yourself!

1523
01:55:05,031 --> 01:55:07,193
Those are the best odds
I've had in years.

1524
01:55:27,554 --> 01:55:29,283
Don't judge me, Cooper.

1525
01:55:30,056 --> 01:55:32,286
You were never tested like I was.

1526
01:55:32,926 --> 01:55:34,416
Few men have been.

1527
01:55:42,736 --> 01:55:44,067
You tried your best.

1528
01:55:53,413 --> 01:55:55,245
You're feeling it, aren't you?

1529
01:55:56,450 --> 01:55:58,282
The survival instinct.

1530
01:55:59,219 --> 01:56:03,554
That's what drove me. It's what drives
all of us. And it's what will save us.

1531
01:56:04,691 --> 01:56:08,059
Because I want to save all of us.
For you, Cooper.

1532
01:56:13,200 --> 01:56:16,761
I'm sorry, I can't watch you
go through this. I'm sorry.

1533
01:56:17,103 --> 01:56:19,765
I thought I could, but I can't.

1534
01:56:20,941 --> 01:56:23,000
I'm here. I'm here for you.

1535
01:56:23,343 --> 01:56:25,334
Just listen to my voice, Cooper.

1536
01:56:26,146 --> 01:56:27,477
I'm right here.

1537
01:56:29,316 --> 01:56:30,647
You're not alone.

1538
01:56:40,360 --> 01:56:42,021
Do you see your children?

1539
01:56:45,131 --> 01:56:46,462
It's okay.

1540
01:56:47,501 --> 01:56:49,162
They're right there with you.

1541
01:56:53,039 --> 01:56:55,804
Did Professor Brand tell you
that poem before you left?

1542
01:56:56,476 --> 01:56:57,966
Do you remember?

1543
01:57:00,347 --> 01:57:03,009
Do not go gentle into that good night

1544
01:57:04,951 --> 01:57:07,784
Old age should burn and rave

1545
01:57:07,988 --> 01:57:09,478
At close of day

1546
01:57:11,057 --> 01:57:13,651
Rage, rage against the dying of the light

1547
01:57:38,084 --> 01:57:39,848
Brand! Help!

1548
01:57:40,053 --> 01:57:41,885
-Help!
-Cooper?

1549
01:57:48,595 --> 01:57:49,790
CASE!

1550
01:57:50,196 --> 01:57:51,493
No air.

1551
01:57:52,299 --> 01:57:53,528
Ammonia.

1552
01:57:54,601 --> 01:57:57,036
Cooper, we're coming! CASE!

1553
01:57:57,304 --> 01:57:59,170
-I have a fix.
-Go! Go!

1554
01:57:59,372 --> 01:58:01,204
And away!

1555
01:58:12,986 --> 01:58:15,182
Cooper, we're coming.
Hang in there. Don't talk.

1556
01:58:15,388 --> 01:58:17,789
Breathe as little as possible.
We're almost there.

1557
01:58:21,361 --> 01:58:23,295
There's a security lockout, sir.

1558
01:58:23,496 --> 01:58:26,761
It requires a person to access function.

1559
01:58:31,805 --> 01:58:33,295
It's all yours, sir.

1560
01:58:41,548 --> 01:58:42,982
Try not to breathe.

1561
01:58:44,017 --> 01:58:45,507
We're coming right now.

1562
01:58:45,819 --> 01:58:47,844
CASE, come on! Come on!

1563
01:58:48,054 --> 01:58:50,819
We got to go faster, CASE!
Faster, faster!

1564
01:58:55,328 --> 01:58:57,057
Hang in there.

1565
01:59:03,570 --> 01:59:06,835
I see him, I see him!
CASE, bank! Bank right!

1566
01:59:25,859 --> 01:59:27,418
Cooper! Cooper!

1567
01:59:27,794 --> 01:59:28,852
I'm here!

1568
01:59:52,118 --> 01:59:54,109
This data makes no sense.

1569
02:00:02,529 --> 02:00:03,758
I'm sorry.

1570
02:00:04,097 --> 02:00:06,691
-What?
-Mann was lying!

1571
02:00:14,474 --> 02:00:16,238
Go. Go.

1572
02:00:17,343 --> 02:00:18,367
Romilly.

1573
02:00:18,745 --> 02:00:19,769
Romilly!

1574
02:00:20,213 --> 02:00:22,545
Do you read me, Romilly?

1575
02:00:23,116 --> 02:00:25,107
Step back, professor! Step back!

1576
02:00:32,926 --> 02:00:35,657
-Romilly...
-Romilly, do you read me?

1577
02:00:38,631 --> 02:00:39,962
Keep watch!

1578
02:00:41,167 --> 02:00:42,259
Lois?

1579
02:00:42,769 --> 02:00:45,363
-What happened to caution, CASE?
-Safety first, Cooper.

1580
02:00:46,673 --> 02:00:50,075
Romilly? Romilly, do you read me?
This is Brand.

1581
02:00:52,946 --> 02:00:53,970
Romilly?

1582
02:00:54,180 --> 02:00:56,740
Dr. Brand. Cooper.
There's been an explosion.

1583
02:00:57,383 --> 02:00:59,181
Dr. Mann's compound.

1584
02:01:26,780 --> 02:01:28,805
TARS, TARS, 10 o'clock!

1585
02:01:35,221 --> 02:01:37,155
Let me know when TARS is aboard.

1586
02:01:40,760 --> 02:01:42,751
Romilly did not survive.

1587
02:01:43,863 --> 02:01:45,331
I could not save him.

1588
02:01:45,532 --> 02:01:47,261
-TARS is in.
-I'll take her from here.

1589
02:01:47,467 --> 02:01:50,164
-Do we have a fix on the Ranger?
-He's pushing into orbit!

1590
02:01:50,370 --> 02:01:53,362
If he takes control of that ship,
we're dead.

1591
02:01:54,607 --> 02:01:57,702
-He'd maroon us?
-He is marooning us.

1592
02:01:59,546 --> 02:02:02,038
I'll meet you guys downstairs.
Go wait by the car.

1593
02:02:09,889 --> 02:02:14,122
Come on! Give me your bag. Get in
the backseat. Get in the backseat now!

1594
02:02:19,766 --> 02:02:21,757
Dr. Mann, please respond.

1595
02:02:22,302 --> 02:02:24,327
Dr. Mann, please respond!

1596
02:02:24,537 --> 02:02:26,733
He doesn't know the Endurance
docking procedure.

1597
02:02:26,940 --> 02:02:29,534
-The autopilot does.
-Not since TARS disabled it.

1598
02:02:30,710 --> 02:02:31,734
Nice.

1599
02:02:31,945 --> 02:02:34,915
-What's your trust setting, TARS?
-Lower than yours apparently.

1600
02:02:38,418 --> 02:02:41,752
Do not attempt docking.
I repeat, do not attempt docking.

1601
02:02:42,088 --> 02:02:43,249
Please res--

1602
02:03:42,582 --> 02:03:44,880
Auto-docking sequence withheld.

1603
02:03:45,151 --> 02:03:46,812
-Override.
-Unauthorized.

1604
02:03:48,488 --> 02:03:49,819
Override.

1605
02:03:50,323 --> 02:03:52,223
Unauthorized.

1606
02:03:56,296 --> 02:04:00,460
Do not attempt docking. I repeat,
do not attempt docking. Please res--

1607
02:04:44,277 --> 02:04:46,439
Moving slowly toward the Endurance.

1608
02:05:11,037 --> 02:05:12,527
Imperfect contact.

1609
02:05:12,872 --> 02:05:14,806
-Override.
-Hatch lockout.

1610
02:05:22,548 --> 02:05:25,142
-Is he locked on yet?
-Imperfectly.

1611
02:05:26,953 --> 02:05:28,182
Dr. Mann, do not--

1612
02:05:49,675 --> 02:05:52,667
Hatch lockout disengaged.

1613
02:06:01,220 --> 02:06:04,713
Dr. Mann, do not,
I repeat, do not open the hatch.

1614
02:06:05,058 --> 02:06:06,685
I repeat, do not open the hatch.

1615
02:06:06,893 --> 02:06:09,919
If you open the hatch,
the airlock will depressurize.

1616
02:06:35,555 --> 02:06:37,990
-What happens if he blows the airlock?
-Nothing good.

1617
02:06:43,963 --> 02:06:46,591
Okay, pull back.
Retro thrusters, everything we've got!

1618
02:06:46,799 --> 02:06:48,460
-Thrusters are full!
-Back!

1619
02:06:48,668 --> 02:06:52,935
Relay my transmission to the computer
and rebroadcast it as emergency P.A.

1620
02:06:53,139 --> 02:06:54,163
Dr. Mann--

1621
02:06:56,809 --> 02:06:59,676
I repeat, do not open the inner hatch.

1622
02:06:59,879 --> 02:07:00,903
Brand?

1623
02:07:01,647 --> 02:07:03,809
I don't know what he said to you...

1624
02:07:04,050 --> 02:07:06,542
...but I am taking command
of the Endurance.

1625
02:07:06,986 --> 02:07:09,148
Then we can talk about
completing the mission.

1626
02:07:12,492 --> 02:07:14,221
Dr. Mann, listen to me.

1627
02:07:14,994 --> 02:07:16,985
This is not about my life.

1628
02:07:17,630 --> 02:07:20,861
Or Cooper's life.
This is about all mankind.

1629
02:07:21,467 --> 02:07:22,957
There is a moment--

1630
02:07:29,242 --> 02:07:30,573
It is not sa--

1631
02:07:38,818 --> 02:07:39,842
Oh, my God.

1632
02:07:58,171 --> 02:08:02,130
-No point using your fuel to chase--
-Analyze the Endurance's spin.

1633
02:08:07,380 --> 02:08:09,644
-Cooper, what are you doing?
-Docking.

1634
02:08:14,253 --> 02:08:16,950
Endurance rotation is 67, 68 RPM.

1635
02:08:17,156 --> 02:08:19,625
Get ready to match our spin
with the retro thrusters.

1636
02:08:19,825 --> 02:08:21,259
-It's not possible.
-No...

1637
02:08:21,994 --> 02:08:23,325
...it's necessary.

1638
02:08:34,907 --> 02:08:37,069
Endurance is hitting stratosphere!

1639
02:08:41,180 --> 02:08:43,171
She's got no heat shield.

1640
02:08:46,285 --> 02:08:48,049
-CASE, you ready?
-Ready.

1641
02:08:56,495 --> 02:08:59,624
Cooper! This is no time for caution.

1642
02:09:01,200 --> 02:09:03,567
CASE, if I black out, you take the stick.

1643
02:09:03,769 --> 02:09:06,295
TARS, get ready to engage
the docking mechanism.

1644
02:09:13,279 --> 02:09:14,906
Endurance is starting to heat.

1645
02:09:15,114 --> 02:09:16,411
Twenty feet out.

1646
02:09:16,782 --> 02:09:19,114
I need three degrees starboard.

1647
02:09:19,418 --> 02:09:21,079
Ten feet out.

1648
02:09:21,320 --> 02:09:23,914
Cooper, we are lined up.

1649
02:09:24,156 --> 02:09:25,646
Initiating spin!

1650
02:10:07,166 --> 02:10:08,395
Come on, TARS.

1651
02:10:16,642 --> 02:10:18,132
Come on, TARS!

1652
02:10:29,255 --> 02:10:31,883
-We are locked, Cooper.
-Locked. Easing up!

1653
02:10:39,532 --> 02:10:40,863
Easy.

1654
02:10:42,034 --> 02:10:43,365
Easy.

1655
02:10:49,208 --> 02:10:50,698
Retro thrusters!

1656
02:10:58,684 --> 02:11:00,174
Main engines on.

1657
02:11:05,925 --> 02:11:08,656
Pushing out of orbit. Come on, baby.

1658
02:11:16,669 --> 02:11:18,330
Killing main engines!

1659
02:11:19,572 --> 02:11:21,233
Okay, we're out of orbit.

1660
02:11:28,581 --> 02:11:31,448
Okay. And for our next trick!

1661
02:11:33,319 --> 02:11:37,153
It'll have to be good.
We're heading into Gargantua's pull.

1662
02:11:38,057 --> 02:11:39,081
Oh, shit.

1663
02:11:39,592 --> 02:11:41,390
-CASE, take the stick.
-Roger that.

1664
02:12:31,343 --> 02:12:33,675
Cooper, we're slipping
towards Gargantua.

1665
02:12:35,014 --> 02:12:36,948
Shall I use main engines?

1666
02:12:37,149 --> 02:12:38,173
No.

1667
02:12:38,784 --> 02:12:41,048
We got to let her slide as far as we can.

1668
02:12:49,829 --> 02:12:52,696
-Give it to me.
-There's good news, there's bad news.

1669
02:12:52,965 --> 02:12:56,629
I've heard that one, TARS.
Give it to me straight.

1670
02:13:05,344 --> 02:13:08,507
The backup generator kicked in,
so the system's stable. All good.

1671
02:13:08,714 --> 02:13:09,738
Good.

1672
02:13:10,182 --> 02:13:13,584
Okay. The navigational hub
has been completely destroyed.

1673
02:13:13,853 --> 02:13:16,686
We don't have enough life support
to make it back to Earth...

1674
02:13:16,889 --> 02:13:18,880
...but we can scratch our way
to Edmunds' planet.

1675
02:13:19,091 --> 02:13:20,388
What about fuel?

1676
02:13:20,826 --> 02:13:23,318
Not enough. But I have a plan.

1677
02:13:23,963 --> 02:13:27,957
We let Gargantua pull us down
close to her horizon.

1678
02:13:28,267 --> 02:13:31,669
Then a powered slingshot around,
launching us towards Edmunds' planet.

1679
02:13:31,871 --> 02:13:34,340
-Manually?
-That's what I'm here for.

1680
02:13:34,540 --> 02:13:36,941
I'm gonna take us
just inside the critical orbit.

1681
02:13:37,142 --> 02:13:39,907
-What about time slippage?
-Neither of us has time to worry...

1682
02:13:40,112 --> 02:13:41,773
...about relativity right now.

1683
02:13:46,385 --> 02:13:47,978
I'm sorry, Cooper.

1684
02:13:51,023 --> 02:13:54,254
Once we've gathered enough speed
around Gargantua...

1685
02:13:54,593 --> 02:13:56,425
...we use Lander 1...

1686
02:13:56,929 --> 02:13:59,227
...and Ranger 2 as rocket boosters to...

1687
02:14:00,065 --> 02:14:02,397
...push us out of the black hole's gravity.

1688
02:14:04,737 --> 02:14:07,365
The Lander's linkages
have been destroyed...

1689
02:14:07,573 --> 02:14:09,905
...so we'll have to control manually.

1690
02:14:11,143 --> 02:14:14,579
Once Lander 1 is spent,
TARS will detach--

1691
02:14:14,747 --> 02:14:16,476
And get sucked into that black hole.

1692
02:14:17,249 --> 02:14:18,580
Why does TARS have to detach?

1693
02:14:18,751 --> 02:14:21,220
We must shed the weight
to escape the gravity.

1694
02:14:21,420 --> 02:14:22,751
Newton's third law.

1695
02:14:23,022 --> 02:14:25,821
The only way humans have ever figured
out of getting somewhere...

1696
02:14:25,991 --> 02:14:27,322
...is to leave something behind.

1697
02:14:27,593 --> 02:14:30,563
Cooper, you can't ask TARS
to do this for us.

1698
02:14:30,763 --> 02:14:34,529
He's a robot. So you don't have to
ask him to do anything.

1699
02:14:34,800 --> 02:14:36,131
Cooper, you asshole!

1700
02:14:36,435 --> 02:14:38,426
Sorry, you broke up a little bit there.

1701
02:14:38,771 --> 02:14:41,035
It's what we intended, Dr. Brand.

1702
02:14:41,440 --> 02:14:43,738
It's our only chance
to save people on Earth.

1703
02:14:43,943 --> 02:14:47,243
If I can find a way to transmit
the quantum data I'll find in there...

1704
02:14:47,446 --> 02:14:49,278
...they might still make it.

1705
02:14:50,349 --> 02:14:52,943
Let's just hope
there's still someone there to save.

1706
02:15:21,447 --> 02:15:23,108
Maximum velocity achieved.

1707
02:15:24,350 --> 02:15:26,682
Prepare to fire escape thrusters.

1708
02:15:26,986 --> 02:15:28,010
Ready?

1709
02:15:28,487 --> 02:15:29,511
Ready.

1710
02:15:29,855 --> 02:15:30,947
Ready.

1711
02:15:31,156 --> 02:15:32,885
Main engine ignition in three...

1712
02:15:33,359 --> 02:15:34,383
...two...

1713
02:15:34,793 --> 02:15:36,818
...one. Mark.

1714
02:15:50,209 --> 02:15:51,472
Come on, baby.

1715
02:15:52,645 --> 02:15:54,807
Lander 1 engines , on my mark.

1716
02:15:55,147 --> 02:15:56,171
Three...

1717
02:15:56,615 --> 02:15:57,639
...two...

1718
02:15:57,850 --> 02:15:59,409
...one. Mark.

1719
02:16:04,757 --> 02:16:07,249
Ranger 2 engines, on my mark.

1720
02:16:07,526 --> 02:16:09,858
Three, two, one...

1721
02:16:10,429 --> 02:16:11,521
-Mark.
-Fire!

1722
02:16:21,707 --> 02:16:24,870
This little maneuver's
gonna cost us 51 years!

1723
02:16:25,778 --> 02:16:28,270
You don't sound so bad
for pushing 120!

1724
02:16:43,662 --> 02:16:46,825
Lander 1, prepare to detach on my mark.

1725
02:16:47,499 --> 02:16:49,490
Three, two...

1726
02:16:50,836 --> 02:16:52,770
...one. Mark.

1727
02:16:52,971 --> 02:16:53,995
Detach!

1728
02:16:57,109 --> 02:16:59,544
-Goodbye, TARS.
-Goodbye, Dr. Brand.

1729
02:16:59,812 --> 02:17:02,804
-See you on the other side, Coop.
-See you there, Slick!

1730
02:17:11,323 --> 02:17:12,882
Okay, CASE.

1731
02:17:13,926 --> 02:17:17,328
-Nice reckless flying!
-Learned from the master.

1732
02:17:17,830 --> 02:17:20,390
-Ranger 2, prepare to detach.
-What?

1733
02:17:20,632 --> 02:17:23,465
No! No! Cooper! What are you doing?

1734
02:17:23,969 --> 02:17:27,405
Newton's third law.
You got to leave something behind.

1735
02:17:27,773 --> 02:17:30,140
You told me we had enough resources
for both of us.

1736
02:17:30,409 --> 02:17:32,070
We agreed, Amelia...

1737
02:17:32,611 --> 02:17:33,942
...90 percent.

1738
02:17:35,848 --> 02:17:36,906
Don't.

1739
02:17:39,118 --> 02:17:40,279
Detach.

1740
02:17:53,398 --> 02:17:56,231
Okay, I am nosing down.

1741
02:17:59,938 --> 02:18:01,929
Approaching the event horizon.

1742
02:18:04,176 --> 02:18:06,440
Portside, dipping down beneath it...

1743
02:18:07,012 --> 02:18:08,673
...to go through it.

1744
02:18:10,282 --> 02:18:13,115
Heading towards blackness.

1745
02:18:13,452 --> 02:18:15,443
I have a visual of the event.

1746
02:18:15,988 --> 02:18:17,319
It's all black.

1747
02:18:19,458 --> 02:18:21,119
TARS, do you read me?

1748
02:18:21,460 --> 02:18:22,950
It's all blackness.

1749
02:18:24,963 --> 02:18:26,954
TARS! Do you read me? Over.

1750
02:18:31,804 --> 02:18:32,965
Okay.

1751
02:18:34,173 --> 02:18:35,436
Screens...

1752
02:18:37,042 --> 02:18:38,532
...getting interference.

1753
02:18:40,279 --> 02:18:42,839
Losing control of the stick. I got flashes.

1754
02:18:43,448 --> 02:18:45,610
Flashes of lightness and blackness.

1755
02:18:46,819 --> 02:18:49,311
The turbulence in the gravity
is increasing.

1756
02:18:55,994 --> 02:18:57,655
The computers are going down.

1757
02:19:09,942 --> 02:19:12,741
Gravitational pull.
I'm losing control of the stick.

1758
02:19:46,044 --> 02:19:47,705
Murph? Murph, come on!

1759
02:19:53,385 --> 02:19:55,376
Eject. Eject.

1760
02:19:56,889 --> 02:19:57,981
Eject.

1761
02:19:58,390 --> 02:19:59,551
Eject.

1762
02:20:00,058 --> 02:20:01,082
Eject.

1763
02:20:01,793 --> 02:20:02,885
Eject.

1764
02:21:55,107 --> 02:21:56,131
Murph.

1765
02:22:06,118 --> 02:22:08,246
Murph! Murph! Murph!

1766
02:22:16,661 --> 02:22:17,685
Murph!

1767
02:22:24,903 --> 02:22:26,735
No, no, no!

1768
02:22:27,005 --> 02:22:28,063
Murph!

1769
02:22:28,407 --> 02:22:29,897
Murph!

1770
02:22:30,108 --> 02:22:31,132
No! No!

1771
02:23:30,869 --> 02:23:32,803
If you're leaving, just go.

1772
02:23:33,305 --> 02:23:34,466
No, no. No.

1773
02:23:36,074 --> 02:23:38,372
No, don't go. Don't go, you idiot.

1774
02:23:38,577 --> 02:23:39,874
Don't go!

1775
02:23:41,813 --> 02:23:43,474
Morse. Morse.

1776
02:23:44,249 --> 02:23:45,273
Morse.

1777
02:23:46,485 --> 02:23:48,283
Dot. Dot.

1778
02:23:48,453 --> 02:23:49,477
S.

1779
02:23:55,627 --> 02:23:56,651
T.

1780
02:24:01,266 --> 02:24:02,290
A.

1781
02:24:02,567 --> 02:24:03,591
Dash!

1782
02:24:06,404 --> 02:24:07,735
Dash! Dash!

1783
02:24:08,273 --> 02:24:09,263
Murph!

1784
02:24:09,441 --> 02:24:11,432
Murph, we don't have time for this!

1785
02:24:11,843 --> 02:24:12,935
Y.

1786
02:24:18,283 --> 02:24:19,307
"Stay!"

1787
02:24:30,395 --> 02:24:32,727
Come on, Murph. Come on!

1788
02:24:33,431 --> 02:24:35,195
What's it say, Murph?

1789
02:24:36,001 --> 02:24:37,059
What's it say?

1790
02:24:47,646 --> 02:24:48,738
"Stay."

1791
02:25:24,282 --> 02:25:25,750
Tell him, Murph.

1792
02:25:25,951 --> 02:25:27,441
Make him stay.

1793
02:25:30,789 --> 02:25:32,450
Make him stay, Murph.

1794
02:25:41,466 --> 02:25:43,366
Make him stay, Murph.

1795
02:25:44,836 --> 02:25:46,930
Don't let me leave, Murph!

1796
02:25:50,208 --> 02:25:52,575
Don't let me leave, Murph!

1797
02:25:58,383 --> 02:26:00,715
No! No! No!

1798
02:26:21,406 --> 02:26:22,896
It was you.

1799
02:26:34,352 --> 02:26:36,411
You were my ghost.

1800
02:26:46,131 --> 02:26:49,123
Cooper. Cooper. Come in, Cooper.

1801
02:26:50,935 --> 02:26:53,427
-TARS?
-Roger that.

1802
02:26:55,240 --> 02:26:57,834
-You survived.
-Somewhere...

1803
02:26:58,376 --> 02:27:00,367
...in their fifth dimension.

1804
02:27:00,645 --> 02:27:02,636
They saved us.

1805
02:27:03,048 --> 02:27:07,884
Yeah? Who the hell is "they"? And just
why would they want to help us?

1806
02:27:08,086 --> 02:27:11,147
I don't know, but they constructed
this three-dimensional space...

1807
02:27:11,356 --> 02:27:14,621
...inside their five-dimensional reality
to allow you to understand it.

1808
02:27:14,826 --> 02:27:16,555
Yeah, that ain't working.

1809
02:27:16,861 --> 02:27:18,192
Yes, it is.

1810
02:27:18,463 --> 02:27:22,127
You've seen that time is represented
here as a physical dimension.

1811
02:27:22,334 --> 02:27:25,326
You have worked out that you can
exert a force across space-time.

1812
02:27:26,104 --> 02:27:29,369
Gravity to send a message.

1813
02:27:29,774 --> 02:27:31,105
Affirmative.

1814
02:27:33,678 --> 02:27:34,804
Gravity...

1815
02:27:35,947 --> 02:27:39,281
...can cross the dimensions,
including time.

1816
02:27:39,617 --> 02:27:40,880
Apparently.

1817
02:27:44,789 --> 02:27:46,780
Do you have the quantum data?

1818
02:27:47,192 --> 02:27:48,819
Roger. I have it.

1819
02:27:49,794 --> 02:27:53,628
I am transmitting it on all wavelengths,
but nothing is getting out.

1820
02:27:53,965 --> 02:27:56,127
I can do this.

1821
02:27:56,434 --> 02:27:58,926
But such complicated data to a child?

1822
02:27:59,137 --> 02:28:00,468
Not just any child.

1823
02:28:00,905 --> 02:28:02,236
What else?

1824
02:28:02,607 --> 02:28:05,599
Oh, come on, Dad.

1825
02:28:07,145 --> 02:28:08,943
Murph, the fire's out! Come on!

1826
02:28:09,147 --> 02:28:13,482
Even if you communicate it, she won't
understand its significance for years.

1827
02:28:14,018 --> 02:28:15,486
I get that, TARS.

1828
02:28:15,687 --> 02:28:20,352
We've got to figure something out or the
people on Earth are gonna die. Think!

1829
02:28:20,658 --> 02:28:21,716
Cooper...

1830
02:28:22,327 --> 02:28:24,989
...they didn't bring us here
to change the past.

1831
02:28:28,366 --> 02:28:29,697
Say that again.

1832
02:28:30,034 --> 02:28:33,698
They didn't bring us here
to change the past.

1833
02:28:37,876 --> 02:28:39,935
No, they didn't bring us here at all.

1834
02:28:44,716 --> 02:28:46,377
We brought ourselves.

1835
02:28:55,627 --> 02:28:59,461
TARS, give me the coordinates
for NASA in binary.

1836
02:29:00,231 --> 02:29:02,666
In binary. Roger. Feeding data.

1837
02:29:26,991 --> 02:29:28,925
"It's not a ghost."

1838
02:29:30,662 --> 02:29:32,152
"It's gravity."

1839
02:29:32,497 --> 02:29:34,158
Don't you get it yet, TARS?

1840
02:29:34,833 --> 02:29:36,631
I brought myself here!

1841
02:29:37,502 --> 02:29:40,995
We're here to communicate
with the three-dimensional world.

1842
02:29:41,439 --> 02:29:42,929
We're the bridge!

1843
02:29:50,949 --> 02:29:52,713
I thought they chose me.

1844
02:29:56,054 --> 02:29:59,718
-They didn't choose me, they chose her.
-For what, Cooper?

1845
02:30:02,260 --> 02:30:03,921
To save the world.

1846
02:30:08,199 --> 02:30:12,602
All of this is one little girl's bedroom.
Every moment.

1847
02:30:12,904 --> 02:30:14,235
It's infinitely complex.

1848
02:30:14,472 --> 02:30:16,873
They have access
to infinite time and space...

1849
02:30:17,041 --> 02:30:18,975
...but they're not bound by anything!

1850
02:30:19,244 --> 02:30:24,080
They can't find a specific place in time.
They can't communicate.

1851
02:30:25,583 --> 02:30:28,382
That's why I'm here.
I'm gonna find a way to tell Murph...

1852
02:30:28,586 --> 02:30:30,179
...just like I found this moment.

1853
02:30:30,388 --> 02:30:32,686
-How, Cooper?
-Love, TARS, love.

1854
02:30:32,891 --> 02:30:37,328
It's just like Brand said. My connection
with Murph it is quantifiable. It's the key!

1855
02:30:37,562 --> 02:30:39,394
What are we here to do?

1856
02:30:40,331 --> 02:30:42,197
Find how to tell her.

1857
02:30:45,003 --> 02:30:46,334
The watch.

1858
02:30:49,440 --> 02:30:50,601
The watch.

1859
02:30:52,010 --> 02:30:53,102
That's it.

1860
02:30:55,613 --> 02:30:58,605
We code the data into the movement
of the second hand.

1861
02:31:02,253 --> 02:31:05,416
TARS, translate the data into Morse
and feed it to me.

1862
02:31:05,623 --> 02:31:06,954
Translating data.

1863
02:31:07,425 --> 02:31:09,917
Cooper, what if
she never came back for it?

1864
02:31:11,596 --> 02:31:14,258
She will. She will.

1865
02:31:15,500 --> 02:31:16,990
Murph, I can see his car!

1866
02:31:18,303 --> 02:31:19,361
He's coming!

1867
02:31:21,306 --> 02:31:23,138
Okay. I'm coming down.

1868
02:31:24,609 --> 02:31:26,099
How do you know?

1869
02:31:29,347 --> 02:31:31,179
Because I gave it to her.

1870
02:31:36,554 --> 02:31:39,046
Roger. Morse is dot-dot-dash-dot.

1871
02:31:42,560 --> 02:31:43,652
Dot-dot...

1872
02:31:45,029 --> 02:31:46,690
...dash-dot.

1873
02:31:47,131 --> 02:31:48,792
Dot-dash-dot-dot.

1874
02:31:49,200 --> 02:31:51,862
Dot-dash-dot-dot.

1875
02:31:52,203 --> 02:31:53,693
Dash-dash-dash.

1876
02:31:54,005 --> 02:31:56,269
Dash, dash, dash.

1877
02:32:14,525 --> 02:32:15,856
He came back!

1878
02:32:16,794 --> 02:32:20,958
It was him all the time!
I didn't know. It was him!

1879
02:32:22,166 --> 02:32:23,998
Dad's gonna save us.

1880
02:33:06,744 --> 02:33:08,007
Eureka!

1881
02:33:11,783 --> 02:33:13,114
It's traditional.

1882
02:33:17,655 --> 02:33:19,316
Eureka!

1883
02:33:24,028 --> 02:33:25,518
Did it work?

1884
02:33:25,830 --> 02:33:27,525
I think it might have.

1885
02:33:28,900 --> 02:33:30,163
How do you know?

1886
02:33:30,401 --> 02:33:33,735
Because the bulk beings
are closing the tesseract.

1887
02:33:37,575 --> 02:33:39,566
Don't you get it yet, TARS?

1888
02:33:40,745 --> 02:33:42,577
They're not "beings."

1889
02:33:43,247 --> 02:33:44,578
They're us.

1890
02:33:46,651 --> 02:33:49,985
What I've been doing for Murph,
they're doing for me.

1891
02:33:50,321 --> 02:33:51,789
For all of us.

1892
02:33:51,989 --> 02:33:54,151
Cooper, people couldn't build this.

1893
02:33:54,425 --> 02:33:56,757
No. No, not yet.

1894
02:33:57,095 --> 02:33:58,585
But one day.

1895
02:33:59,263 --> 02:34:02,597
Not you and me. But people.

1896
02:34:03,301 --> 02:34:07,204
A civilization that's evolved
past the four dimensions we know.

1897
02:34:13,778 --> 02:34:15,268
What happens now?

1898
02:35:17,909 --> 02:35:19,343
Mr. Cooper.

1899
02:35:23,548 --> 02:35:25,277
Let's take it slow, sir.

1900
02:35:25,683 --> 02:35:27,742
Nice and easy, Mr. Cooper.

1901
02:35:28,019 --> 02:35:30,954
Remember, you're no spring chicken
anymore. Actually, you are...

1902
02:35:31,122 --> 02:35:33,022
...124 years old.

1903
02:35:40,031 --> 02:35:41,362
Take it slow, sir.

1904
02:35:42,433 --> 02:35:44,595
You were extremely lucky.

1905
02:35:44,936 --> 02:35:48,270
The Rangers found you with only
minutes left in your oxygen supply.

1906
02:36:02,420 --> 02:36:03,751
Where am I?

1907
02:36:05,489 --> 02:36:06,979
Cooper Station.

1908
02:36:08,459 --> 02:36:09,949
Currently orbiting Saturn.

1909
02:36:11,996 --> 02:36:13,987
Cooper Station.

1910
02:36:16,467 --> 02:36:18,561
Nice of you to name it after me.

1911
02:36:22,273 --> 02:36:23,297
What?

1912
02:36:23,574 --> 02:36:26,737
The station isn't named after you.
It's named after your daughter.

1913
02:36:27,245 --> 02:36:30,271
Although she's always maintained
just how important you were.

1914
02:36:30,481 --> 02:36:32,142
Is she still alive?

1915
02:36:34,085 --> 02:36:35,985
She'll be here in a couple weeks.

1916
02:36:37,021 --> 02:36:39,922
She is far too old to be transferring
from another station...

1917
02:36:40,091 --> 02:36:42,025
...but when she heard
you'd been found...

1918
02:36:43,261 --> 02:36:45,923
...well, this is Murphy Cooper
we're talking about.

1919
02:36:46,831 --> 02:36:48,321
Yes, it is.

1920
02:36:50,434 --> 02:36:52,926
We'll have you checked out of here
in a couple days.

1921
02:36:55,606 --> 02:36:57,938
I'm sure you'll be excited to see
what's in store.

1922
02:36:59,510 --> 02:37:03,344
I actually did a paper
on you in high school.

1923
02:37:03,648 --> 02:37:05,980
I know all about your life back on Earth.

1924
02:37:07,685 --> 02:37:08,948
Oh, yeah.

1925
02:37:09,220 --> 02:37:10,244
Right.

1926
02:37:13,357 --> 02:37:18,022
If you follow me, we've got
a really good situation for you.

1927
02:37:21,332 --> 02:37:25,394
So when I made my suggestion
to Miss Cooper...

1928
02:37:26,304 --> 02:37:28,773
...I was delighted to hear
she thought it was perfect.

1929
02:37:32,476 --> 02:37:35,810
It was just constant.
Just that steady blow of dirt.

1930
02:37:36,147 --> 02:37:38,844
Of course,
I didn't speak to her personally.

1931
02:37:39,050 --> 02:37:40,711
We always set the plate upside-down.

1932
02:37:40,885 --> 02:37:43,911
Glasses or cups, whatever it was,
upside-down.

1933
02:37:44,155 --> 02:37:48,991
My dad was a farmer.
Like everybody else back then.

1934
02:37:49,226 --> 02:37:51,558
There just wasn't enough food.

1935
02:37:51,829 --> 02:37:54,560
We wore little things, little strips...

1936
02:37:54,832 --> 02:37:59,827
...of sheet over our nose and mouth
so we wouldn't breathe so much of it.

1937
02:38:00,037 --> 02:38:03,439
Well, it was pretty exciting for me
because it was hope.

1938
02:38:03,708 --> 02:38:07,872
I don't care who describes it, there is no
way to exaggerate it. It was that bad.

1939
02:38:08,079 --> 02:38:11,743
She did confirm
just how much you loved farming.

1940
02:38:12,049 --> 02:38:13,710
-Oh, she did, did she?
-Yeah.

1941
02:38:13,884 --> 02:38:15,113
Come here.

1942
02:38:15,686 --> 02:38:20,681
Home sweet home. Everything
replaced and put back where it...

1943
02:38:27,031 --> 02:38:29,022
-Hey, is this...?
-Oh, yeah.

1944
02:38:29,233 --> 02:38:32,225
The machine we found out near Saturn
when we found you. Yes.

1945
02:38:32,436 --> 02:38:35,201
Its power source was shot,
but we could get you another one.

1946
02:38:35,406 --> 02:38:37,397
Yes. Please.

1947
02:38:38,642 --> 02:38:39,973
Settings.

1948
02:38:40,478 --> 02:38:42,139
General settings.

1949
02:38:42,480 --> 02:38:44,414
Security settings.

1950
02:38:47,118 --> 02:38:49,450
Honesty, new setting:

1951
02:38:50,554 --> 02:38:52,044
95 percent.

1952
02:38:52,390 --> 02:38:54,882
Confirmed. Additional customization?

1953
02:38:55,793 --> 02:38:56,885
Humor:

1954
02:38:58,562 --> 02:38:59,893
75 percent.

1955
02:39:00,297 --> 02:39:01,628
Confirmed.

1956
02:39:02,266 --> 02:39:05,361
Auto self-destruct T-minus 10, nine...

1957
02:39:05,603 --> 02:39:07,594
Let's make that 60 percent.

1958
02:39:09,807 --> 02:39:11,969
Sixty percent confirmed.

1959
02:39:12,643 --> 02:39:15,738
-Knock-knock.
-You want 55?

1960
02:39:20,518 --> 02:39:22,612
Is this really what it was like?

1961
02:39:24,922 --> 02:39:27,254
It was never this clean, Slick.

1962
02:39:31,762 --> 02:39:35,426
I don't care much for this pretending
we're back where we started.

1963
02:39:37,635 --> 02:39:39,797
I want to know where we are.

1964
02:39:41,505 --> 02:39:42,995
Where we're going.

1965
02:39:47,545 --> 02:39:50,537
-Mr. Cooper. The family's all in there.
-Yeah.

1966
02:39:50,815 --> 02:39:51,839
Family?

1967
02:39:52,049 --> 02:39:55,917
They all came out to see her. She's been
in cryo-sleep for almost two years.

1968
02:40:28,986 --> 02:40:31,478
You told them I like farming.

1969
02:40:36,827 --> 02:40:38,488
It was me, Murph.

1970
02:40:41,098 --> 02:40:42,759
I was your ghost.

1971
02:40:44,168 --> 02:40:45,658
I know.

1972
02:40:47,671 --> 02:40:52,074
People didn't believe me.
They thought I was doing it all myself.

1973
02:40:52,376 --> 02:40:53,400
But...

1974
02:40:56,280 --> 02:40:58,214
...I knew who it was.

1975
02:41:03,254 --> 02:41:05,188
Nobody believed me.

1976
02:41:05,990 --> 02:41:08,288
But I knew you'd come back.

1977
02:41:10,694 --> 02:41:11,923
How?

1978
02:41:14,031 --> 02:41:17,023
Because my dad promised me.

1979
02:41:23,107 --> 02:41:25,098
Well, I'm here now, Murph.

1980
02:41:26,377 --> 02:41:27,708
I'm here.

1981
02:41:29,313 --> 02:41:30,508
No.

1982
02:41:32,783 --> 02:41:36,720
No parent should have to watch
their own child die.

1983
02:41:39,657 --> 02:41:42,319
I have my kids here for me now.

1984
02:41:45,095 --> 02:41:46,585
You go.

1985
02:41:49,967 --> 02:41:51,162
Where?

1986
02:41:54,939 --> 02:41:56,270
Brand.

1987
02:42:05,916 --> 02:42:07,577
She's out there...

1988
02:42:13,991 --> 02:42:15,823
...setting up camp.

1989
02:42:42,786 --> 02:42:44,117
Alone...

1990
02:42:45,623 --> 02:42:47,785
...in a strange galaxy.

1991
02:42:58,702 --> 02:43:03,367
Maybe right now
she's settling in for the long nap...

1992
02:43:21,825 --> 02:43:24,556
...by the light of our new sun...

1993
02:43:40,010 --> 02:43:42,069
...in our new home.
